window.BOOK_NETWORKS_CHAPTERS = {
  "README.md": "# Computer Networking & Web Protocols for Junior Coders 🌐🔌\n\nWelcome to the official **Computer Networking & Web Protocols Textbook** of the Junior Coders curriculum!\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 Course Overview\n\nBehind every click, API call, and video stream is the global nervous system of the Internet. This textbook demystifies how data travels across the planet—from raw physical pulses of light to high-level protocols like HTTP/3, WebSockets, and modern cryptographic handshakes.\n\nIn this textbook, students will:\n\n1. **Master Core Networking Foundations**: Packets, switches, routers, MAC vs IP, LAN vs WAN, and the physical wire.\n2. **Deconstruct Layer Models**: Mental models and layer responsibilities of the OSI 7-Layer and TCP/IP 4-Layer architectures.\n3. **Understand Addressing & Subnetting**: IPv4, IPv6, CIDR notation, subnet calculations, private IP ranges, and NAT.\n4. **Compare Transport Protocols**: TCP (handshakes, reliability, flow control) vs UDP (speed, datagrams, streaming).\n5. **Explore the Domain Name System (DNS)**: Hierarchy of root servers, TLDs, authoritative nameservers, and resource records.\n6. **Master HTTP & Web Architectures**: HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 (QUIC), status codes, headers, wire format, cookies, and caching.\n7. **Secure the Wire with Cryptography (*Amānah*)**: Symmetric vs asymmetric encryption, TLS 1.3 handshakes, certificates, and CORS.\n8. **Diagnose & Build Real Distributed Systems**: Command-line tools (`ping`, `traceroute`, `curl`, `netstat`), DevTools Network tab, and real-time WebSockets.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Curriculum Roadmap\n\n| Part | Focus | Chapters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Part 1** | Network Foundations & Layer Models | Ch 1 - Ch 3 |\n| **Part 2** | Transport Layer, Ports & Sockets | Ch 4 - Ch 5 |\n| **Part 3** | The Domain Name System (DNS) | Ch 6 |\n| **Part 4** | HTTP Evolution & Web Protocols | Ch 7 - Ch 9 |\n| **Part 5** | Securing the Wire (TLS, HTTPS & CORS) | Ch 10 - Ch 11 |\n| **Part 6** | Modern Web, Diagnostics & Capstone | Ch 12 - Ch 14 |\n\n---\n\n## 🌟 Islamic Ethical Framework (*Amānah & Iḥsān*)\n\nAll lessons in this curriculum adhere to our core ethical standards:\n\n- **Digital Privacy as a Sacred Trust (*Amānah*)**: Communications across the wire must be guarded. Plaintext data handling without encryption when handling user secrets violates this sacred trust.\n- **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**: Network skills must be channeled toward connecting communities, advancing education, providing emergency humanitarian relief, and ensuring fair access.\n- **Strictly Halal Contexts**: No projects or simulations involving interest/usury (*Ribā*), gambling/betting platforms (*Maysir*), fraudulent traffic generation, or invasive spyware.\n- **Craftsmanship & Diligence (*Iḥsān*)**: Build resilient, high-uptime, bandwidth-efficient networks with clean architecture and comprehensive error handling.\n",
  "part-01-network-foundations/ch01-how-the-internet-works.md": "# Chapter 1: How the Internet Actually Works 🌐\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nMaryam wants to send a 500-page science report to her cousin in another country. The post office only accepts postcards. So she tears out each page and writes three things on it: her address, her cousin's address and a page number. The postcards travel on different vans and planes. Some arrive early and some arrive late. Her cousin sorts them by page number and rebuilds the whole report. That is exactly how the Internet moves your messages.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain how **packet switching** breaks data into small pieces and rebuilds it.\n- Name the parts of a network: clients, servers, network cards, switches and routers.\n- Tell the difference between a **MAC address** and an **IP address**.\n- Describe the three ways bits travel: electricity, light and radio waves.\n- Run `ping` and read its output line by line.\n\n---\n\n## 📦 The Big Idea: Packet Switching\n\nA **network** is two or more devices joined together so they can share data. The **Internet** is a network of networks. It joins millions of smaller networks across the world.\n\nWhen you send a file, your computer does not send it in one huge piece. It cuts the file into small **packets**. A packet is a small block of data, usually up to about 1,500 bytes. Each packet carries a label, just like Maryam's postcards:\n\n| On the postcard | In a packet |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Maryam's address | **Source address** |\n| Cousin's address | **Destination address** |\n| Page number | **Sequence number** |\n\nDifferent packets may take different roads. The receiving computer puts them back in order. This idea is called **packet switching**.\n\nWhy do this? Two reasons:\n\n1. **Sharing.** Many people can use the same cable at once. One big download does not block everyone else.\n2. **Repair.** If one packet is lost, only that small piece is sent again, not the whole file.\n\nHere is the journey a packet takes from your desk to a website:\n\n```\n[ Your computer ]\n       │  small packets\n       ▼\n[ Wi-Fi access point or switch ]\n       │\n       ▼\n[ Router at home or school ]\n       │\n       ▼\n[ Internet Service Provider (ISP) ]\n       │\n       ▼\n[ Undersea cables and exchange points ]\n       │\n       ▼\n[ Web server in a data centre ]\n```\n\nAn **ISP** (Internet Service Provider) is the company that connects your home or school to the Internet.\n\n---\n\n## 🧱 The Building Blocks of a Network\n\n### Clients and Servers\n\n- A **client** is a device that asks for something. Your laptop, phone or browser is a client.\n- A **server** is a computer that waits for requests and answers them. It usually runs all day and all night.\n\nWhen you open a library website, your browser is the client. The library's computer that sends the page is the server.\n\n### Network Card and MAC Address\n\nEvery device that joins a network has a **Network Interface Card** (NIC). This is the chip that sends and receives signals. Each NIC has a **MAC address** (Media Access Control address). It is a 48-bit number, written as six pairs of hexadecimal digits:\n\n```\nExample MAC address:  00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E\n```\n\nThe factory gives each card its MAC address. Many phones and laptops now show a random MAC address to each Wi-Fi network, to protect your privacy.\n\n### IP Address (a first look)\n\nAn **IP address** is a number that tells the Internet *where* a device is, like a postal address. For example, `203.0.113.10`. Your IP address usually changes when you move from home Wi-Fi to school Wi-Fi. Chapter 3 explains IP addresses in detail.\n\n| | MAC address | IP address |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Who gives it | Card maker (or the device) | The network you join |\n| Used for | Delivery inside one local network | Delivery across networks |\n| Postal idea | The name on your door | Your street address |\n\n### Switches and Routers\n\n- A **switch** joins devices inside one **Local Area Network** (LAN), such as a school computer lab. It sends each packet to the right device using MAC addresses.\n- A **router** joins *different* networks together. It links your LAN to the **Wide Area Network** (WAN), which is the wider Internet. It chooses where to send packets using IP addresses.\n\n```\n Lab PC1   Lab PC2   Lab PC3\n    │         │         │\n    └────┬────┴────┬────┘\n         │ SWITCH  │   (inside the LAN)\n         └────┬────┘\n              │\n          [ ROUTER ]   (joins LAN to WAN)\n              │\n          Internet\n```\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ The Physical Wire: How Bits Move\n\nComputers speak in **bits**: `1`s and `0`s. Bits must travel as real signals. There are three main ways:\n\n1. **Electricity (copper Ethernet cable).** Changes in voltage stand for `1`s and `0`s. One cable run works up to about 100 metres.\n2. **Light (fibre-optic cable).** Laser flashes travel through glass threads thinner than a hair. Light in glass moves at about 200,000 km per second. Cables on the ocean floor carry almost all traffic between continents.\n3. **Radio waves (Wi-Fi and mobile 4G/5G).** Antennas send signals through the air. Radio is easy to use but can be slowed by walls and distance.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: `ping`\n\nThe `ping` command sends a few tiny test packets to another computer. That computer replies. `ping` then shows how long each **round trip** took: from you, to the server, and back again.\n\nOn Windows, open **Command Prompt** or **PowerShell** and type:\n\n```\nping example.org\n```\n\nExample output (addresses changed to documentation addresses; yours will differ):\n\n```\nPinging example.org [203.0.113.10] with 32 bytes of data:\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=20ms TTL=60\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=18ms TTL=60\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=19ms TTL=60\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=20ms TTL=60\n\nPing statistics for 203.0.113.10:\n    Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 4, Lost = 0 (0% loss),\nApproximate round trip times in milli-seconds:\n    Minimum = 18ms, Maximum = 20ms, Average = 19ms\n```\n\nHow to read it:\n\n- `[203.0.113.10]` is the IP address that the name `example.org` turned into.\n- `bytes=32` is the size of each test packet.\n- `time=20ms` is the round-trip time in **milliseconds** (thousandths of a second).\n- `Lost = 0` means every packet came back.\n- Ignore `TTL` for now. Chapter 13 explains it.\n\nOn macOS or Linux, `ping` keeps going until you press **Ctrl + C**. Use `ping -c 4 example.org` to send just four.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ Digital Stewardship (*Amānah*): The Shared Commons\n\nThe Internet works only because thousands of universities, companies and countries agree on the same open rules. These rules are called **protocols**. As young developers, we treat this shared network as a trust (*Amānah*):\n\n- We build pages that load quickly and do not waste data.\n- We design for people on slow or patchy rural connections.\n- We respect the privacy of every packet that passes by.\n- We only test networks we own, or that our teacher allows.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Thinking `ping` time is one-way.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong idea: \"`time=20ms` means my packet reached the server in 20 ms.\"\n- What really happens: 20 ms is the **whole round trip**, there and back.\n- ✅ Right idea: \"The trip there took roughly half, about 10 ms, and the reply took the rest.\"\n\n**Thinking \"no reply\" always means \"the server is down\".**\n\n- ❌ Wrong idea: `Request timed out.` means the website is broken.\n- What really happens: many servers and firewalls block ping on purpose. The website may still open fine in a browser.\n- ✅ Right idea: if ping fails, also try the website in a browser before deciding.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nPing `example.org` four times and write down the **Minimum**, **Maximum** and **Average** times.\nThen move further from the Wi-Fi access point and ping again. Did the times change?\n\n> 💡 Hint: on Windows, `ping -n 4 example.org` sends exactly four packets. At school, only ping addresses your teacher allows.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- The Internet is a network of networks that moves data in small **packets**.\n- Packets carry a source, a destination and a sequence number, so they can be rebuilt in order.\n- **Clients** ask; **servers** answer.\n- A **switch** connects devices inside a LAN using MAC addresses; a **router** joins networks using IP addresses.\n- Bits travel as electricity, light or radio waves.\n- `ping` measures the **round-trip** time to another computer.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is **packet switching**, and why do computers split large files into small packets?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nPacket switching means cutting data into small packets that travel separately and are rebuilt at the other end.\nIt lets many people share the same cables at once, and if one packet is lost, only that small piece needs to be sent again.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA student runs `ping example.org` and sees `Lost = 0 (0% loss)` and `Average = 40ms`.\n1. Did all packets come back?\n2. Roughly how long did the trip *to* the server take?\n3. Which device connected the student's school LAN to the Internet?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Yes. `Lost = 0` means every packet returned.\n2. About 20 ms. The 40 ms average is the round trip, so one way is roughly half.\n3. The router. It joins the LAN to the WAN.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nDraw your own school network as a text diagram.\n1. Show at least three devices in one lab.\n2. Add the switch that joins them.\n3. Add the router and the ISP.\n4. Label which device uses MAC addresses and which uses IP addresses to choose where packets go.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```\n PC1      PC2      Printer\n  │        │         │\n  └───┬────┴────┬────┘\n      │ SWITCH  │  uses MAC addresses\n      └───┬─────┘\n          │\n      [ ROUTER ]   uses IP addresses\n          │\n      [  ISP  ]\n          │\n       Internet\n```\n\nThe switch delivers packets inside the lab using MAC addresses. The router sends packets out to other networks using IP addresses.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-network-foundations/ch02-osi-and-tcpip-models.md": "# Chapter 2: The OSI & TCP/IP Layer Models 🧱\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThink of a school sending a book parcel to a sister school abroad. The librarian chooses the book and writes a note. A clerk wraps it and numbers the parcels. The post office stamps the city and postcode. A van driver carries it along the road. Each person does one job and passes the parcel on. Nobody needs to know how the others do their work. Networks are built in the same way, in **layers**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why networks are split into **layers**.\n- List the seven layers of the **OSI model** in order.\n- Match the four layers of the **TCP/IP model** to the OSI layers.\n- Describe **encapsulation**: how each layer wraps the data with its own header.\n- Use layers to find where a network problem is.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 Why Layers?\n\nWhen your computer talks to a server far away, many details must work together. There are electrical signals, error checks, addresses, routes and data formats. That is too much for one program to handle.\n\nSo engineers split the work into **layers**. A layer is one group of jobs. Each layer:\n\n- does one kind of work,\n- takes help from the layer **below** it,\n- offers a service to the layer **above** it.\n\nThis means one layer can change without breaking the others. For example, you can switch from a cable to Wi-Fi, and your browser still works the same way.\n\n---\n\n## 🏛️ The OSI 7-Layer Model\n\nThe **OSI model** (Open Systems Interconnection) is a teaching model made by ISO, an international standards group. It describes network work in seven layers. Layer 1 is at the bottom, closest to the wire.\n\n| Layer | Name | Main job | Data unit | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **7** | Application | Network services for programs | Data | HTTP, DNS, SSH, SMTP |\n| **6** | Presentation | Format, compress, encrypt data | Data | JSON, gzip, JPEG, TLS* |\n| **5** | Session | Open, keep and close conversations | Data | session setup |\n| **4** | Transport | Delivery between programs, ports | Segment / Datagram | TCP, UDP |\n| **3** | Network | Addresses and routes across networks | Packet | IP, ICMP, routers |\n| **2** | Data Link | Delivery to the next device on the local link | Frame | Ethernet, Wi-Fi, MAC, switches |\n| **1** | Physical | Bits as signals | Bits | Cables, fibre, radio |\n\n\\* TLS does not fit neatly into one OSI layer. Many books place it at layer 6. You will meet it in Chapter 10.\n\nMost names in the table are taught later in this book. For now, just notice which layer each one lives in.\n\n> 💡 **Memory trick (top to bottom):**\n> **A**ll **P**eople **S**eem **T**o **N**eed **D**ata **P**rocessing.\n> (Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical)\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 The TCP/IP 4-Layer Model\n\nThe OSI model is great for learning. The real Internet uses a simpler model with four layers. It is called the **TCP/IP model**, after its two most famous protocols.\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────┐\n│ Application  HTTP, DNS, SSH  │ OSI 5-7\n├──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Transport    TCP, UDP        │ OSI 4\n├──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Internet     IP, ICMP        │ OSI 3\n├──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Link         Ethernet, Wi-Fi │ OSI 1-2\n└──────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\n| TCP/IP layer | Covers OSI layers | Question it answers |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Application | 5, 6, 7 | *What* is the message? |\n| Transport | 4 | *Which program* gets it? |\n| Internet | 3 | *Which computer* on Earth? |\n| Link | 1, 2 | *Which next device* on this wire? |\n\n---\n\n## 📦 Encapsulation: Wrapping the Data\n\nWhen you send a message, the data moves **down** the layers on your computer. Each layer adds a **header**. A header is a small block of instructions for the same layer on the other computer. This wrapping is called **encapsulation**.\n\n```\nApp data:      [ Salam ]\n                   │ + TCP header\n                   ▼\nSegment:   [TCP|Salam]\n                   │ + IP header\n                   ▼\nPacket:  [IP|TCP|Salam]\n                   │ + frame header\n                   ▼    and trailer\nFrame: [ETH|IP|TCP|Salam|FCS]\n```\n\n- The **TCP header** holds port numbers (Chapter 5) and sequence numbers (Chapter 4).\n- The **IP header** holds the source and destination IP addresses (Chapter 3).\n- The **Ethernet header** holds MAC addresses. The **trailer** holds a check value (FCS) to spot damaged frames.\n\nThe receiving computer does the opposite. It removes one header at each layer, going **up**. This is called **decapsulation**. In the end, only the message `Salam` reaches the program.\n\n> 📏 A normal Ethernet frame can carry up to 1,500 bytes of payload. This limit is called the **MTU** (Maximum Transmission Unit). Those 1,500 bytes include the IP and TCP headers, not just your data.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: Layers in `ipconfig`\n\nOn Windows, run this in Command Prompt or PowerShell:\n\n```\nipconfig /all\n```\n\nExample output (shortened; addresses changed to documentation and sample values; yours will differ):\n\n```\nWireless LAN adapter Wi-Fi:\n\n   Description . . . . . . . : Wireless Network Adapter\n   Physical Address. . . . . : 00-1A-2B-3C-4D-5E\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . : 192.168.1.7(Preferred)\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . : 192.168.1.1\n   DNS Servers . . . . . . . : 192.0.2.53\n```\n\nEach line belongs to a layer:\n\n- `Physical Address` is the MAC address: **Link layer** (OSI 2).\n- `IPv4 Address`, `Subnet Mask` and `Default Gateway` belong to the **Internet layer** (OSI 3).\n- `DNS Servers` is used by an **Application layer** service (OSI 7).\n\nOn macOS or Linux, try `ip addr` (Linux) or `ifconfig` (macOS).\n\n---\n\n## 🩺 Using Layers to Find Problems\n\nNetwork engineers often check layers from the bottom up. Fix the lowest broken layer first.\n\n| What you see | Most likely layer |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Cable unplugged, Wi-Fi turned off | Physical (1) |\n| Connected to Wi-Fi, but no IP address | Network (3), often because the address service failed |\n| `ping` works, but the website gives an error page | Application (7) |\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Mixing up the names of the data units.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"The switch reads the packet's MAC address.\"\n- What really happens: MAC addresses live in the **frame** header (layer 2). A packet is the layer 3 unit.\n- ✅ Right: \"The switch reads the **frame's** MAC address. The router reads the **packet's** IP address.\"\n\n**Thinking the OSI model is what computers actually run.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"My laptop has seven separate network programs, one per layer.\"\n- ✅ Right: OSI is a map for learning and talking about problems. Real systems follow the TCP/IP model.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun `ipconfig /all` on your computer. Copy four lines from it and label each one with its layer (Link, Internet or Application).\n\n> 💡 Hint: anything with \"Address\" made of six pairs like `00-1A-...` is a MAC address. Four numbers with dots are IP addresses.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Layers split hard network work into small jobs that can change on their own.\n- **OSI** has 7 layers: Application, Presentation, Session, Transport, Network, Data Link, Physical.\n- **TCP/IP** has 4 layers: Application, Transport, Internet, Link.\n- **Encapsulation** adds a header at each layer going down; **decapsulation** removes them going up.\n- Segment = layer 4, packet = layer 3, frame = layer 2.\n- When troubleshooting, start at the bottom layer.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nAt which OSI layer do HTTP, DNS and SSH work? And what is the data unit called at layers 4, 3 and 2?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nHTTP, DNS and SSH work at the **Application layer** (layer 7).\nLayer 4 = **segment** (TCP) or **datagram** (UDP), layer 3 = **packet**, layer 2 = **frame**.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nMatch each problem to the most likely layer:\n1. A laptop's network cable is cut.\n2. The laptop has no IP address after joining Wi-Fi.\n3. The website loads but shows `500 Internal Server Error`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. Physical layer (1): the signal cannot travel.\n2. Network layer (3): without an IP address, packets cannot be addressed.\n3. Application layer (7): the network works, but the web program on the server failed.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA student sends the message `Hello` to a web server.\n1. Draw the frame that leaves the computer, showing every header in order.\n2. Say which device reads the outer header first: a switch or a router?\n3. Explain what the receiving computer does with the headers.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The frame looks like this:\n\n```\n[ETH|IP|TCP|Hello|FCS]\n```\n\n2. The **switch** reads the Ethernet header (MAC addresses) first, because it works at layer 2. A router later reads the IP header.\n3. The receiver does **decapsulation**. It checks and removes the Ethernet header and trailer, then the IP header, then the TCP header. Only `Hello` reaches the program.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-01-network-foundations/ch03-ip-addresses-and-subnets.md": "# Chapter 3: IP Addresses, Subnets & CIDR 🗺️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA letter needs a full address: the city, the street and the house number. The sorting office first looks at the city and street. Only the local postman cares about the house number. Computer addresses work the same way. One part of an IP address names the **network** (the street). The other part names the **device** (the house).\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Read an **IPv4 address** in decimal and in binary.\n- Explain why **IPv6** was created and how it is written.\n- Tell **private** addresses from **public** ones and explain **NAT**.\n- Use a **subnet mask** and **CIDR** notation (like `/24`) to split an address.\n- Work out the network address, broadcast address and usable hosts of a subnet.\n\n---\n\n## 🔢 IPv4: Four Numbers, 32 Bits\n\nAn **IPv4 address** is 32 bits long. We split it into four groups of 8 bits, called **octets**. Each octet is written as a normal number, with dots in between. This is called **dotted-decimal** notation.\n\n```\nBinary:  11000000.10101000.00000001.00000111\nDecimal:      192.     168.       1.       7\n```\n\nOne octet has 8 bits, so it can hold 2⁸ = 256 values: from `0` to `255`. A number like `300` can never appear in an IPv4 address.\n\nAll together, IPv4 has 2³² = 4,294,967,296 addresses. That is about 4.3 billion.\n\n### Converting one octet\n\nEach bit in an octet has a place value:\n\n```\n128  64  32  16   8   4   2   1\n  1   1   0   0   1   0   1   1\n```\n\nAdd the place values where the bit is `1`: 128 + 64 + 8 + 2 + 1 = **203**. So `11001011` = 203.\n\n---\n\n## 🌍 IPv6: A Much Bigger Address Book\n\nIPv4 was designed in 1981. Nobody imagined phones, laptops and sensors in every home. The free IPv4 addresses ran out.\n\n**IPv6** is the answer:\n\n- It uses **128 bits**.\n- It is written as 8 groups of 4 **hexadecimal** digits (0–9 and a–f), with colons.\n- It gives 2¹²⁸ addresses, about 3.4 × 10³⁸. That is more than enough for every device we can imagine.\n\n```\nFull:   2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001\nShort:  2001:db8::1\n```\n\nTwo rules make it shorter: drop leading zeros in a group, and replace **one** run of all-zero groups with `::`. The `2001:db8::` range is kept for examples in books like this one.\n\n---\n\n## 🏠 Private and Public Addresses, and NAT\n\nSome IPv4 ranges are **private**. They are free to use inside any home, school or office. Routers on the Internet never forward them.\n\n| Private range | CIDR | Typical use |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `10.0.0.0` – `10.255.255.255` | `10.0.0.0/8` | Large organisations |\n| `172.16.0.0` – `172.31.255.255` | `172.16.0.0/12` | Medium networks |\n| `192.168.0.0` – `192.168.255.255` | `192.168.0.0/16` | Homes and small offices |\n\nA **public** address is unique on the whole Internet. Your ISP usually gives your router one public address, for example `203.0.113.88`.\n\n**NAT** (Network Address Translation) lets many private devices share that one public address. When your laptop sends a packet out, the router swaps the private source address for its public one. It writes the swap in a table. When the reply comes back, it looks up the table and passes the reply to the right device.\n\n```\nLaptop  192.168.1.15 ─┐\nTablet  192.168.1.20 ─┼─► Router (NAT)\nPrinter 192.168.1.30 ─┘   public IP:\n                          203.0.113.88\n                               │\n                               ▼\n                           Internet\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✂️ Subnet Masks and CIDR\n\nEvery IP address has two parts:\n\n1. The **network ID**: which network (the street).\n2. The **host ID**: which device on that network (the house).\n\nA **subnet mask** shows where one part ends and the other begins. Its `1` bits cover the network ID. Its `0` bits cover the host ID.\n\n```\nMask 255.255.255.0 in binary:\n11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000\n└──── 24 ones: network ───┘└ host ┘\n```\n\n**CIDR** (Classless Inter-Domain Routing) is a short way to write the mask. You count the `1` bits and write them after a slash. So `255.255.255.0` is written `/24`.\n\n```\n192.168.1.7/24\n└─network─┘ └ host\n 192.168.1    .7\n```\n\n### Quick CIDR table\n\nA `/n` prefix leaves (32 − n) host bits. Total addresses = 2 to the power of the host bits.\n\n| CIDR | Subnet mask | Total addresses | Usable hosts |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `/24` | `255.255.255.0` | 256 | 254 |\n| `/25` | `255.255.255.128` | 128 | 126 |\n| `/26` | `255.255.255.192` | 64 | 62 |\n| `/28` | `255.255.255.240` | 16 | 14 |\n| `/30` | `255.255.255.252` | 4 | 2 (a link between two routers) |\n\n> ⚠️ **Why subtract 2?**\n> 1. The **first** address (all host bits `0`) is the **network address**. It names the subnet itself.\n> 2. The **last** address (all host bits `1`) is the **broadcast address**. A packet sent there reaches every device in the subnet.\n\n### Worked example: splitting a /24 into four /26 subnets\n\nA school has the block `198.51.100.0/24`. It wants four equal subnets: one each for the office, library, science lab and computer lab. Moving from /24 to /26 borrows 2 bits, so 2² = 4 subnets of 64 addresses each.\n\nThese results were checked with Python's `ipaddress` module:\n\n```\nSubnet             First host     Last host       Broadcast\n198.51.100.0/26    198.51.100.1   198.51.100.62   198.51.100.63\n198.51.100.64/26   198.51.100.65  198.51.100.126  198.51.100.127\n198.51.100.128/26  198.51.100.129 198.51.100.190  198.51.100.191\n198.51.100.192/26  198.51.100.193 198.51.100.254  198.51.100.255\n```\n\nEach subnet has 64 addresses and 62 usable hosts.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: Your Own Address\n\nOn Windows, run:\n\n```\nipconfig\n```\n\nExample output (shortened; yours will differ):\n\n```\nWireless LAN adapter Wi-Fi:\n\n   IPv6 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 2001:db8:5c22::7\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.1.7\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.1.1\n```\n\n- `IPv4 Address` is your **private** address on this LAN.\n- `Subnet Mask 255.255.255.0` means your network is a `/24`.\n- `Default Gateway` is your router. Packets for other networks go there.\n\nTo show only the useful lines in Command Prompt, try `ipconfig | findstr /i \"ipv4 mask gateway\"`.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Giving a device the network or broadcast address.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: setting a computer to `192.168.1.0` or `192.168.1.255` in a `/24` network.\n- What happens: these two addresses are reserved, so the device cannot talk normally.\n- ✅ Right: use a host address from `192.168.1.1` to `192.168.1.254`.\n\n**Thinking a bigger prefix means a bigger network.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"/28 is bigger than /24 because 28 > 24.\"\n- ✅ Right: a bigger prefix leaves **fewer** host bits. A /28 has 16 addresses; a /24 has 256.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun `ipconfig` and find your IPv4 address and subnet mask. Then answer:\n\n1. Is your address private? Which range is it in?\n2. What is your network's CIDR prefix?\n3. What would the broadcast address of your network be?\n\n> 💡 Hint: for mask `255.255.255.0`, keep the first three octets and put `255` in the last one.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- IPv4 addresses have 32 bits in four octets, each from 0 to 255.\n- IPv6 addresses have 128 bits, written in hexadecimal groups like `2001:db8::1`.\n- Private ranges (`10.x`, `172.16–31.x`, `192.168.x`) are used inside networks; **NAT** shares one public address.\n- A subnet mask, or CIDR prefix like `/24`, splits the address into network ID and host ID.\n- Usable hosts = total addresses − 2 (network and broadcast).\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nHow many bits are in an IPv4 address and in an IPv6 address? What does NAT do?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nIPv4 has **32 bits**; IPv6 has **128 bits**.\nNAT lets many devices with private addresses share one public address. The router swaps the addresses and remembers each conversation in a table.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nFor the subnet `192.168.10.0/24`, write the network address, the broadcast address and the range of usable host addresses.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- Network address: `192.168.10.0`\n- Broadcast address: `192.168.10.255`\n- Usable hosts: `192.168.10.1` to `192.168.10.254` (254 hosts)\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA community library has the block `203.0.113.0/24`. It needs two equal subnets: one for staff and one for public reading-room computers.\n1. Which prefix gives exactly two equal subnets?\n2. Write both subnets with their first host, last host and broadcast address.\n3. How many usable hosts does each subnet have?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. `/25`. Borrowing 1 bit gives 2¹ = 2 subnets of 128 addresses.\n2. The subnets:\n\n```\n203.0.113.0/25    hosts .1  – .126  broadcast .127\n203.0.113.128/25  hosts .129 – .254 broadcast .255\n```\n\n3. Each has 128 − 2 = **126** usable hosts.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-transport-and-ports/ch04-tcp-vs-udp.md": "# Chapter 4: Transport Protocols: TCP vs. UDP 🚚\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nImagine two ways a school shares news. The first is a signed letter to each parent. Every page is numbered, and the parent signs a slip to say it arrived. If a page is missing, the office sends it again. The second is an announcement over the school loudspeaker. It is instant, but if you missed a word, nobody repeats it. Networks have both kinds of delivery: **TCP** is the signed letter, and **UDP** is the loudspeaker.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what the **Transport layer** (layer 4) adds on top of IP.\n- Describe how **TCP** makes delivery reliable with the **3-way handshake**, sequence numbers and acknowledgements.\n- Explain why **UDP** is lighter and faster but gives no guarantees.\n- Choose TCP or UDP for a given app.\n- Read the connection list from `netstat`.\n\n---\n\n## 🚦 What the Transport Layer Does\n\nIP (layer 3) gets packets from one computer to another. But IP is **best effort**. That means it tries, but packets can be lost, copied twice or arrive out of order. IP does not fix this.\n\nThe **Transport layer** sits on top of IP. It has two main protocols:\n\n- **TCP** (Transmission Control Protocol): careful and reliable.\n- **UDP** (User Datagram Protocol): light and fast.\n\nBoth also carry **port numbers**, which tell the computer *which program* gets the data. Chapter 5 is all about ports.\n\n---\n\n## 🤝 TCP: The Reliable Courier\n\nBefore TCP sends any real data, the two computers agree to talk. This agreement is called the **3-way handshake**. It uses three small messages:\n\n- **SYN** (synchronise): \"Can we talk? My numbers start at 1000.\"\n- **SYN-ACK** (synchronise + acknowledge): \"Yes. I got 1000. My numbers start at 5000.\"\n- **ACK** (acknowledge): \"Got it. Let's begin.\"\n\n```\n Client                    Server\n   │   1. SYN  seq=1000       │\n   ├─────────────────────────►│\n   │   2. SYN-ACK             │\n   │      seq=5000 ack=1001   │\n   │◄─────────────────────────┤\n   │   3. ACK  ack=5001       │\n   ├─────────────────────────►│\n   │                          │\n ESTABLISHED          ESTABLISHED\n   │ ======= data =========►  │\n```\n\nThe **ack** number means \"I have everything up to here; send the next byte from this number\".\n\n### What TCP promises\n\n1. **Reliable delivery.** The receiver sends **acknowledgements** (ACKs). If the sender gets no ACK in time, it sends the data again.\n2. **In-order delivery.** Every byte has a **sequence number**. Data that arrives out of order is put back in order.\n3. **Flow control.** The receiver says how much it can take. A fast sender cannot flood a slow receiver.\n4. **Congestion control.** TCP slows down when the network looks busy, so everyone gets a fair share.\n\nWhen the conversation ends, each side sends a **FIN** (finish) message to close the connection politely.\n\nTCP's header is **20 to 60 bytes**. That is the cost of all these features.\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 UDP: The Lightweight Messenger\n\nUDP skips the handshake, the ACKs and the resending. It adds a tiny **8-byte header** and sends the data at once. A UDP message is called a **datagram**.\n\n```\n┌──────────────┬──────────────┐\n│ Source port  │ Dest. port   │\n│   16 bits    │   16 bits    │\n├──────────────┼──────────────┤\n│ Length       │ Checksum     │\n│   16 bits    │   16 bits    │\n└──────────────┴──────────────┘\n   UDP header = 8 bytes total\n```\n\nThe **checksum** lets the receiver spot damaged data. But UDP will not ask for it again. If the app needs a missing piece, the app must handle it.\n\nWhy would anyone want that? Because in some apps, **late data is useless**. In a live voice call, a word that arrives one second late is worse than a tiny gap. Waiting for a resend would make the sound freeze.\n\n---\n\n## ⚖️ When to Use TCP or UDP\n\n| Feature | TCP | UDP |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Connection | Handshake first | No handshake |\n| Reliability | Lost data is resent | Lost data stays lost |\n| Order | Always in order | May arrive out of order |\n| Speed to start | Slower (1 round trip first) | Instant |\n| Header size | 20–60 bytes | 8 bytes |\n| Good for | Web pages, email, file downloads, online forms | Voice and video calls, live online lessons, DNS lookups, HTTP/3 (QUIC) |\n\nYou will meet DNS in Chapter 6 and HTTP/3 in Chapter 8. HTTP/3 runs over UDP but builds its own reliability on top, so it gets speed *and* safety.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: `netstat`\n\n`netstat` lists the TCP and UDP connections on your computer. On Windows, run:\n\n```\nnetstat -an\n```\n\n- `-a` shows all connections and listening ports.\n- `-n` shows numbers instead of names, which is faster.\n\nExample output (shortened; addresses changed to documentation addresses; yours will differ):\n\n```\nActive Connections\n\n  Proto  Local Address        Foreign Address      State\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:135          0.0.0.0:0            LISTENING\n  TCP    192.168.1.7:52814    203.0.113.10:443     ESTABLISHED\n  TCP    192.168.1.7:52820    198.51.100.25:443    TIME_WAIT\n  UDP    0.0.0.0:5353         *:*\n```\n\nHow to read it:\n\n- `Proto` is the protocol: TCP or UDP.\n- `LISTENING` means a program is waiting for new TCP connections.\n- `ESTABLISHED` means the handshake finished and data can flow.\n- `TIME_WAIT` means the connection was just closed. Windows waits a short time before reusing it.\n- UDP lines have **no state**, because UDP has no connections.\n\nTo show only active TCP connections, use `netstat -an | findstr ESTABLISHED`.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Thinking UDP is \"bad\" because it is unreliable.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"Always use TCP, it is safer.\"\n- What happens: a live voice call over plain TCP can freeze while it waits for one lost packet.\n- ✅ Right: choose by the app's needs. Use TCP when every byte matters. Use UDP when fresh data matters more than perfect data.\n\n**Thinking TCP makes the network itself reliable.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"With TCP, packets never get lost.\"\n- ✅ Right: packets still get lost on the network. TCP *notices* the loss and sends them again.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen a web page in your browser, then quickly run `netstat -an | findstr ESTABLISHED`.\nCount how many connections use remote port `443`. Then close the browser, wait a minute, and run it again. What changed?\n\n> 💡 Hint: the remote port is the number after the colon in the `Foreign Address` column.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- IP is best effort; the Transport layer adds ports and, with TCP, reliability.\n- TCP starts with a 3-way handshake: **SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK**.\n- TCP uses sequence numbers and ACKs to resend lost data and keep order.\n- UDP has an 8-byte header, no handshake and no resending: fast but no promises.\n- Pick TCP for web pages and files; pick UDP for live voice, video and DNS.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nName the three messages of the TCP 3-way handshake, in order. How big is a UDP header?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**SYN**, then **SYN-ACK**, then **ACK**.\nA UDP header is **8 bytes**. A TCP header is 20 to 60 bytes.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nFor each app, choose TCP or UDP and give one reason:\n1. Downloading a PDF of the school timetable.\n2. A live voice call between two classrooms.\n3. Submitting a charity donation form.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. **TCP**: every byte of the PDF must arrive, or the file is broken.\n2. **UDP**: late sound is useless; a tiny gap is better than a freeze.\n3. **TCP**: the form data must arrive complete and exactly once in order.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA client starts a TCP handshake with sequence number `4000`. The server chooses `9000`.\n1. Write the three handshake messages with their `seq` and `ack` numbers.\n2. The client then sends 100 bytes of data starting at its next sequence number. What `ack` number will the server reply with?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The handshake:\n\n```\n1. SYN      seq=4000\n2. SYN-ACK  seq=9000  ack=4001\n3. ACK      seq=4001  ack=9001\n```\n\n2. The data covers bytes 4001 to 4100. The server replies with **ack=4101**, meaning \"send byte 4101 next\".\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-02-transport-and-ports/ch05-ports-and-sockets.md": "# Chapter 5: Ports & Sockets: The Doorways of the Operating System 🚪\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA parcel arrives at a big block of flats. The street address gets the van to the front door. But the building has hundreds of flats. The flat number on the parcel tells the caretaker exactly whose door to knock on. Your computer is that building. The IP address finds the computer, and the **port number** finds the right program inside it.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what a **port** is and why one IP address needs many of them.\n- Define a **socket** and a **socket pair**.\n- Name the three port ranges and some well-known ports.\n- Find which program is using a port with `netstat -ano` and `tasklist`.\n- Explain how a **firewall** protects ports.\n\n---\n\n## 🔢 One Address, Many Programs\n\nRight now, your computer may be running many network programs at once:\n\n- a browser with several tabs open,\n- a Quran recitation app playing audio,\n- a code editor talking to GitHub,\n- a local web server such as Apache in XAMPP.\n\nAll of them share **one** IP address. So how does the computer know which incoming data belongs to which program? Each program uses a different **port number**.\n\nA **port** is a number from `0` to `65,535`. It is 16 bits long, so 2¹⁶ = 65,536 values are possible. Ports are part of the TCP and UDP headers you met in Chapter 4.\n\n---\n\n## 🔌 Sockets and Socket Pairs\n\nA **socket** is one end of a conversation. It is an IP address plus a port:\n\n**Socket = IP address : port**\n\n- `203.0.113.10:443` is a web server's socket.\n- `192.168.1.7:52814` is your laptop's socket for one browser connection.\n\nA TCP connection is named by **both** ends. This is called a **socket pair**:\n\n```\nClient socket          Server socket\n192.168.1.7:52814 ◄──► 203.0.113.10:443\n```\n\nEach new connection from your browser uses a different client port. So the server can tell all your tabs apart, even though they come from the same IP address.\n\n---\n\n## 🏷️ The Three Port Ranges\n\n**IANA** (Internet Assigned Numbers Authority) is the group that keeps the official list of ports. It splits them into three ranges.\n\n### Well-known ports (0 – 1,023)\n\nUsed by core Internet services. On Linux, a program usually needs administrator rights to listen on these ports.\n\n| Port | Protocol | Used for |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 20, 21 | FTP | Older file transfer |\n| 22 | SSH | Secure remote login |\n| 25 | SMTP | Sending email between servers |\n| 53 | DNS | Turning names into IP addresses |\n| 80 | HTTP | Web pages (not encrypted) |\n| 443 | HTTPS | Web pages (encrypted) |\n\n### Registered ports (1,024 – 49,151)\n\nUsed by apps, databases and development servers. Some common ones:\n\n| Port | Usually used by |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| 3000 | Node.js development servers |\n| 3306 | MySQL and MariaDB databases |\n| 5432 | PostgreSQL database |\n| 8000 / 8080 | Other web servers, e.g. `php artisan serve` uses 8000 |\n\nThese are habits, not rules. Any program can be set to use another free port.\n\n### Dynamic or ephemeral ports (49,152 – 65,535)\n\n**Ephemeral** means short-lived. When your browser opens a connection, the operating system picks a free port from this range for it. When the connection closes, the port is freed. This is the IANA range and the Windows default; Linux uses a slightly different range.\n\n```\nYour laptop                 Server\nport 52814 ──── request ───► port 443\nport 52814 ◄─── reply ────── port 443\n(ephemeral)              (well-known)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: Who Is Using Port 80?\n\nIf XAMPP's Apache will not start, another program may already be using port 80. On Windows, run:\n\n```\nnetstat -ano | findstr :80\n```\n\n- `-o` adds the **PID** (Process ID), the number Windows gives each running program.\n\nExample output (yours will differ):\n\n```\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:80      0.0.0.0:0     LISTENING    9096\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:8080    0.0.0.0:0     LISTENING    4908\n  TCP    [::]:80         [::]:0        LISTENING    9096\n```\n\n- `0.0.0.0:80` means \"listening on port 80 on every IPv4 address of this computer\".\n- `[::]:80` is the same for IPv6.\n- `9096` is the PID of the program.\n\nNow find the program's name:\n\n```\ntasklist /FI \"PID eq 9096\"\n```\n\nExample output:\n\n```\nImage Name               PID Session Name   Mem Usage\n==================== ======= ============ ===========\nhttpd.exe               9096 Console        17,488 K\n```\n\n`httpd.exe` is Apache. So Apache is the program on port 80.\n\n### Testing a port on a server\n\nPowerShell can test whether a server accepts TCP connections on a port:\n\n```\nTest-NetConnection example.org -Port 443\n```\n\nExample output (addresses changed to documentation addresses):\n\n```\nComputerName     : example.org\nRemoteAddress    : 203.0.113.10\nRemotePort       : 443\nInterfaceAlias   : Wi-Fi\nSourceAddress    : 192.168.1.7\nTcpTestSucceeded : True\n```\n\n`TcpTestSucceeded : True` means the handshake worked, so something is listening on port 443. Only test servers you own or that your teacher allows.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ Port Security & Firewalls (*Amānah*)\n\nEvery open, listening port is a door. If a database port is open to the whole Internet, strangers can knock on it.\n\nA **firewall** is a guard that checks each packet's addresses and ports. It allows some and blocks others. A common safe setup:\n\n- ✅ Allow port `443` from anyone, so people can visit the website.\n- ❌ Block port `3306` from the Internet, so only the web server itself can reach the database.\n\nKeeping unused doors shut protects the data people trust us with. That is part of our *Amānah*.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Searching for `:80` and getting the wrong port.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `netstat -ano | findstr :80` and deciding that PID `4908` uses port 80.\n- What happens: `findstr :80` also matches `:8080`, `:8000` and even IPv6 addresses that contain `:80`.\n- ✅ Right: look carefully at the whole port number, or search with a space after it: `findstr \":80 \"`.\n\n**Thinking the client also uses port 443.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"My browser sends from port 443 to port 443.\"\n- ✅ Right: the server listens on 443. Your browser uses a temporary **ephemeral** port, such as 52814.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nStart Apache in the XAMPP Control Panel. Then run `netstat -ano | findstr \":80 \"` and `tasklist` with the PID you find.\nDid you see `httpd.exe`? Now stop Apache and run `netstat` again. What disappeared?\n\n> 💡 Hint: if XAMPP is not installed, look for port `443` connections in `netstat -an` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- A **port** (0–65,535) picks the program; the IP address picks the computer.\n- A **socket** is `IP:port`; a connection is a **socket pair**.\n- Well-known ports are 0–1,023 (80 HTTP, 443 HTTPS, 22 SSH, 53 DNS).\n- Clients use short-lived **ephemeral** ports; servers listen on fixed ports.\n- `netstat -ano` and `tasklist` show which program owns a port.\n- Firewalls keep unneeded ports closed.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is a socket? Give the standard ports for HTTP, HTTPS and SSH.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA socket is an IP address plus a port number, for example `192.168.1.7:8080`.\nHTTP uses port **80**, HTTPS uses **443** and SSH uses **22**.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nRead this line from `netstat -an`:\n\n```\nTCP  192.168.1.7:51200  198.51.100.25:443  ESTABLISHED\n```\n\n1. Which side is the client? How do you know?\n2. What kind of service is the server probably running?\n3. Is port 51200 well-known, registered or ephemeral?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `192.168.1.7` is the client. It uses a high, temporary port, and it connects *to* the well-known port 443.\n2. HTTPS, a secure website, because port 443 is the HTTPS port.\n3. Ephemeral (49,152 – 65,535).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nYour school's web server runs a website and a MySQL database on the same machine.\n1. List the two ports it listens on.\n2. Write two firewall rules: one allow, one block.\n3. Explain why the second rule protects students' data.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Port **443** (HTTPS website) and port **3306** (MySQL).\n2. Rules:\n\n```\nALLOW  TCP 443   from anywhere\nBLOCK  TCP 3306  from the Internet\n```\n\n3. The website code talks to MySQL from inside the same machine, so outsiders never need port 3306. Blocking it stops strangers from trying to log in to the database where student records are kept.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-03-the-domain-system/ch06-dns-the-internets-phonebook.md": "# Chapter 6: DNS — The Internet's Global Phonebook 📖\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nBilal wants a copy of *The Canon of Medicine* by Ibn Sina. He asks the librarian at the front desk. She checks her notes first. If the answer is not there, she phones the national library, which sends her to the medicine section, which sends her to the special collection. Finally someone says, \"Shelf 4, Row B.\" She tells Bilal and writes it in her notes for the next student. That is how **DNS** finds the address of a website.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why we need **DNS** to turn names into IP addresses.\n- Read a domain name from right to left and name its parts.\n- Describe the four kinds of DNS servers in a lookup.\n- Recognise common record types: **A**, **AAAA**, **CNAME**, **MX**, **TXT** and **NS**.\n- Explain **TTL** and caching, and look up records with `nslookup`.\n\n---\n\n## 🤔 Why Do We Need DNS?\n\nPeople remember names, like `example.org`. Routers only understand numbers, like `203.0.113.10` (Chapter 3).\n\nThe **Domain Name System** (DNS) connects the two. It is a huge, shared database spread across many servers in the world. When you type a name, DNS finds the matching IP address, usually in a few milliseconds.\n\nDNS lookups usually travel over **UDP port 53** (Chapters 4 and 5). UDP fits well: a question and an answer are small, and a quick retry is easy if one is lost. Large answers can use TCP port 53 instead.\n\n---\n\n## 🌳 Reading a Domain Name\n\nA domain name is read from **right to left**, from the most general part to the most specific.\n\n```\nlearn . school . example . org .\n  │       │        │       │   │\n  │       │        │       │   └ root\n  │       │        │       └ TLD\n  │       │        └ domain\n  │       └ subdomain\n  └ sub-subdomain\n```\n\n- The **root** is written as a dot at the very end. We usually leave it out.\n- The **TLD** (Top-Level Domain) is the last word, like `.org`, `.com` or a country code like `.pk`.\n- The **domain** is the name an organisation registers, like `example.org`.\n- Anything further left is a **subdomain**, which the owner can create freely.\n\nThe DNS tree looks like this:\n\n```\n              . (root)\n         ┌────┴─────┐\n       .com       .org\n                    │\n               example.org\n                    │\n            school.example.org\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🗂️ The Four Servers in a Lookup\n\n1. **Recursive resolver.** This is the \"front desk librarian\". Your ISP, school or a public service runs it. It does all the asking for you.\n2. **Root name server.** It knows where every TLD lives. There are 13 root server names (`a.root-servers.net` to `m.root-servers.net`). They are copied onto more than a thousand machines around the world. Your question goes to the nearest copy, a trick called **anycast**.\n3. **TLD name server.** For `.org`, it knows which servers hold the records for `example.org`.\n4. **Authoritative name server.** It holds the real, official records for the domain. It gives the final answer.\n\n```\n You ──► Resolver ──► Root\n            │  \"ask .org\"\n            ├────────► .org TLD\n            │  \"ask ns1.example.org\"\n            ├────────► Authoritative\n            │  \"203.0.113.10\"\n You ◄──────┘  (answer is cached)\n```\n\nThe resolver saves each answer in its **cache** (short-term memory). The next person who asks gets the answer at once.\n\n---\n\n## 📑 Common DNS Record Types\n\nA domain's settings are stored as **records**. Each record has a type.\n\n| Type | What it does | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A** | Name → IPv4 address | `example.org` → `203.0.113.10` |\n| **AAAA** | Name → IPv6 address | `example.org` → `2001:db8::10` |\n| **CNAME** | Name → another name (an alias) | `www.example.org` → `example.org` |\n| **MX** | Where email for the domain goes | `mail.example.org`, preference 10 |\n| **TXT** | Free text, often to prove you own a domain or protect email | `\"v=spf1 mx -all\"` |\n| **NS** | Which servers are authoritative | `ns1.example.org` |\n\nFor MX records, a **lower** preference number means \"try this mail server first\".\n\n---\n\n## ⏱️ TTL: Time To Live and Caching\n\nEvery record comes with a **TTL** (Time To Live). It is a number of seconds. It tells resolvers how long they may keep the answer in their cache.\n\n- `TTL 3600` means \"you may reuse this for 1 hour\".\n- `TTL 300` means \"check again after 5 minutes\".\n\nA long TTL makes lookups faster and reduces traffic. A short TTL means changes spread faster.\n\n> 💡 **Developer tip:** Before moving a website to a new server, lower its TTL to `300` a day or two early. When you change the IP address, most resolvers will notice within minutes.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: `nslookup`\n\nOn Windows, run in Command Prompt or PowerShell:\n\n```\nnslookup example.org\n```\n\nExample output (addresses changed to documentation addresses; yours will differ):\n\n```\nServer:  UnKnown\nAddress:  192.168.1.1\n\nNon-authoritative answer:\nName:    example.org\nAddresses:  2001:db8::10\n          203.0.113.10\n```\n\nHow to read it:\n\n- `Server` and `Address` show **your resolver**. Here it is the home router. `UnKnown` only means the resolver has no name of its own.\n- `Non-authoritative answer` means the answer came from a resolver's cache, not straight from the official server.\n- `Addresses` lists the AAAA (IPv6) and A (IPv4) records.\n\nTo ask for one record type, add `-type=`:\n\n```\nnslookup -type=mx school.example.org\n```\n\nExample output (sample domain):\n\n```\nNon-authoritative answer:\nschool.example.org  MX preference = 10, mail exchanger = mail.school.example.org\n```\n\n### Seeing the TTL in PowerShell\n\nPowerShell's `Resolve-DnsName` also shows the TTL:\n\n```\nResolve-DnsName example.org -Type A\n```\n\nExample output (addresses changed to documentation addresses):\n\n```\nName          Type   TTL   Section    IPAddress\n----          ----   ---   -------    ---------\nexample.org   A      113   Answer     203.0.113.10\n```\n\nRun it twice, a few seconds apart. The TTL counts **down**, because the resolver is reusing its cached copy.\n\nWindows keeps its own small DNS cache too. `ipconfig /displaydns` shows it, and `ipconfig /flushdns` clears it.\n\nOn macOS and Linux, you can also use `dig example.org`.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Expecting a DNS change to appear everywhere at once.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"I changed my A record. Why do some students still reach the old server?\"\n- What happens: resolvers keep the old answer until its TTL runs out.\n- ✅ Right: lower the TTL before the change, then wait. On your own PC, `ipconfig /flushdns` clears the local copy.\n\n**Pointing a CNAME at an IP address.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `www.example.org  CNAME  203.0.113.10`\n- ✅ Right: a CNAME must point to a **name**. Use an A record for an IP address: `www.example.org  A  203.0.113.10`.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun `nslookup example.org` and `nslookup -type=ns example.org`.\nWrite down your resolver's address, the A and AAAA answers, and the names of the authoritative servers.\n\n> 💡 Hint: the NS answer lines look like `example.org  nameserver = ...`.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- DNS turns names into IP addresses, mostly over UDP port 53.\n- Names are read right to left: root, TLD, domain, subdomain.\n- A lookup goes resolver → root → TLD → authoritative server, and the answer is cached.\n- A = IPv4, AAAA = IPv6, CNAME = alias, MX = mail, TXT = text, NS = name servers.\n- **TTL** says how many seconds an answer may be cached.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between an **A** record and a **CNAME** record? Which server gives the final, official answer?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nAn **A** record maps a name straight to an IPv4 address. A **CNAME** maps a name to *another name*.\nThe **authoritative name server** gives the final, official answer.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA record has `TTL 7200`. A resolver fetched it at 09:00.\n1. Until what time may the resolver reuse it?\n2. The owner changes the IP address at 09:30. When will this resolver see the new address at the latest?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. 7200 seconds = 2 hours, so until **11:00**.\n2. At the latest **11:00**, when the cached copy expires and the resolver asks again.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA charity, `relief.example.org`, is setting up its DNS. Write the records it needs:\n1. The website on IPv4 `198.51.100.20` and IPv6 `2001:db8::20`.\n2. `www.relief.example.org` as an alias of the main name.\n3. Email delivered to `mail.relief.example.org`.\n4. Choose a TTL for each and explain your choice.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n```\nrelief.example.org      A      198.51.100.20\nrelief.example.org      AAAA   2001:db8::20\nwww.relief.example.org  CNAME  relief.example.org\nrelief.example.org      MX 10  mail.relief.example.org\n```\n\nTTL `3600` (1 hour) for all of them is a good normal choice: fast for visitors, and changes still spread within an hour.\nBefore a planned move, lower the A and AAAA TTL to `300`.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-http-and-the-web/ch07-anatomy-of-http-request-and-response.md": "# Chapter 7: Anatomy of an HTTP Request & Response 📬\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nZainab fills in a book request slip at the library desk. The slip has a clear layout: what she wants, her name and class, and a box for notes. The librarian replies on a return slip: \"Found\", \"Not in stock\" or \"Ask at the other desk\", plus the book itself. Because both slips always follow the same layout, any librarian can handle any request. The web works the same way, using **HTTP**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Name the parts of an HTTP **request**: request line, headers, blank line and body.\n- Name the parts of an HTTP **response**: status line, headers, blank line and body.\n- Choose the right **HTTP method** (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) and explain **safe** and **idempotent**.\n- Read the five classes of **status codes**.\n- Watch a real HTTP conversation with `curl.exe -v`.\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 What Is HTTP?\n\n**HTTP** (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) is the language browsers and web servers use. It is an Application-layer protocol (Chapter 2). It usually runs over a TCP connection to port 80 (Chapters 4 and 5).\n\nEvery exchange has two halves:\n\n```\n Browser (client)          Web server\n       │   HTTP request        │\n       ├──────────────────────►│\n       │   HTTP response       │\n       │◄──────────────────────┤\n```\n\nHTTP/1.1 is plain text, so people can read it. Its secure version, **HTTPS**, wraps the same messages in encryption. Chapter 10 explains how.\n\n---\n\n## 📨 The HTTP Request\n\nA request has four parts:\n\n1. **Request line**: the method, the path and the HTTP version.\n2. **Headers**: `Name: value` lines with extra information.\n3. **A blank line**: it marks the end of the headers.\n4. **Body** (optional): data being sent, such as a form.\n\nExample request: a donation form sends JSON (JavaScript Object Notation, a text format for data):\n\n```http\nPOST /api/donations HTTP/1.1\nHost: relief.example.org\nUser-Agent: Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64)\nContent-Type: application/json\nContent-Length: 42\n\n{\"cause\":\"Clean Water Wells\",\"amount\":250}\n```\n\n- `POST` is the method. `/api/donations` is the path on the server.\n- `Host` names the website. One server can host many websites, so this header is required in HTTP/1.1.\n- `Content-Type` says the body is JSON.\n- `Content-Length: 42` is the size of the body in bytes. That body is exactly 42 characters long.\n\nIn the raw bytes, each line ends with two characters: **CR LF** (carriage return, line feed), written `\\r\\n`. The blank line is just `\\r\\n` on its own.\n\n---\n\n## 🏷️ HTTP Methods\n\nThe **method** says what the client wants to do.\n\n| Method | Purpose | Safe? | Idempotent? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET` | Read data | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n| `POST` | Create something or start an action | ❌ No | ❌ No |\n| `PUT` | Replace a whole record | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |\n| `PATCH` | Change some fields of a record | ❌ No | ❌ Not guaranteed |\n| `DELETE` | Remove something | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |\n| `HEAD` | Like GET, but headers only, no body | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n| `OPTIONS` | Ask what is allowed | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n\n- **Safe** means the request should only read. It must not change anything on the server.\n- **Idempotent** means sending it once or ten times leaves the server in the same final state.\n\n> 💡 **Example:** `DELETE /books/42` removes book 42. Sending it again does not remove anything more. But sending `POST /donations` five times could record five donations! That is why browsers warn you before resending a form.\n\n---\n\n## 📬 The HTTP Response\n\nThe server's reply also has four parts:\n\n1. **Status line**: the HTTP version, a **status code** and a short reason.\n2. **Headers**: information about the reply.\n3. **A blank line**.\n4. **Body**: the HTML page, JSON, image or file.\n\nExample response:\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 201 Created\nDate: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 09:20:00 GMT\nServer: Apache/2.4.58 (Win64)\nContent-Type: application/json; charset=UTF-8\nContent-Length: 42\nConnection: keep-alive\n\n{\"status\":\"success\",\"receipt\":\"REC-98214\"}\n```\n\n- `201 Created` means the donation record was made.\n- `Content-Type` tells the browser how to read the body.\n- `Connection: keep-alive` means the TCP connection stays open for the next request (Chapter 8).\n\n---\n\n## 🚦 The Five Classes of Status Codes\n\nA status code is a 3-digit number. The first digit tells you the class.\n\n| Class | Meaning | Common codes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `1xx` | Information, keep going | `101 Switching Protocols` |\n| `2xx` | Success | `200 OK`, `201 Created`, `204 No Content` |\n| `3xx` | Look somewhere else | `301 Moved Permanently`, `302 Found`, `304 Not Modified` |\n| `4xx` | The **client** made a mistake | `400 Bad Request`, `401 Unauthorized`, `403 Forbidden`, `404 Not Found`, `429 Too Many Requests` |\n| `5xx` | The **server** failed | `500 Internal Server Error`, `502 Bad Gateway`, `503 Service Unavailable`, `504 Gateway Timeout` |\n\nA few need care:\n\n- `401 Unauthorized` really means \"not logged in\". The server does not know who you are.\n- `403 Forbidden` means \"I know who you are, but you are not allowed\".\n- `304 Not Modified` means \"your saved copy is still good\" (Chapter 9).\n- `502` and `504` come from a **gateway**, a server that passes requests on to another server behind it.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: `curl.exe -v`\n\n`curl` is a command-line tool that speaks HTTP. It comes with Windows 10 and 11.\n\nIn Windows PowerShell 5, plain `curl` is a nickname (alias) for a different command, `Invoke-WebRequest`. Always type **`curl.exe`** to get the real curl.\n\n```\ncurl.exe -v -o NUL http://example.org/\n```\n\n- `-v` (verbose) shows the request and response headers.\n- `-o NUL` throws the page body away, so the screen stays tidy.\n\nExample output (shortened; addresses changed to documentation addresses; yours will differ):\n\n```\n*   Trying 203.0.113.10:80...\n* Connected to example.org (203.0.113.10) port 80\n> GET / HTTP/1.1\n> Host: example.org\n> User-Agent: curl/8.21.0\n> Accept: */*\n>\n< HTTP/1.1 200 OK\n< Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:39:53 GMT\n< Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8\n< Connection: keep-alive\n< Last-Modified: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:18:28 GMT\n```\n\nHow to read it:\n\n- Lines with `*` are curl's own notes. `Trying ...:80` is the TCP connection to port 80.\n- Lines with `>` are the **request** that curl sent.\n- The empty `>` line is the blank line that ends the headers.\n- Lines with `<` are the **response**. `200 OK` means success.\n\nTo print only the response headers, use `curl.exe -I http://example.org/`. It sends a `HEAD` request.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Using GET to change data.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: a link like `GET /books/42/delete`.\n- What happens: browsers, link checkers and search engines follow GET links freely. One visit could delete the book.\n- ✅ Right: use `DELETE /books/42` (or a `POST` from a form). Keep GET for reading only.\n\n**Mixing up 401 and 403.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: returning `401` to a logged-in student who opens the teachers' page.\n- ✅ Right: return `403 Forbidden`. The server knows who they are; they just lack permission.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun `curl.exe -I http://example.org/` and `curl.exe -I http://example.org/no-such-page`.\nWrite down the status line of each. Which header tells you what kind of content the page is?\n\n> 💡 Hint: look for the first line starting with `HTTP/1.1`, and for `Content-Type`.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- HTTP is the Application-layer language of the web, usually over TCP port 80.\n- A request = request line + headers + blank line + optional body.\n- A response = status line + headers + blank line + body.\n- GET and HEAD are **safe**; GET, PUT and DELETE are **idempotent**; POST is neither.\n- Status codes: 1xx info, 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.\n- Use `curl.exe` (not `curl`) in Windows PowerShell 5.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat separates the headers from the body in an HTTP message? What is the difference between `401` and `403`?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nA **blank line** (a lone `\\r\\n`, so the headers end with `\\r\\n\\r\\n`).\n`401 Unauthorized` means \"log in first: I don't know who you are\". `403 Forbidden` means \"I know who you are, but you are not allowed\".\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nChoose the method and a likely success status code for each:\n1. Show the list of library books.\n2. Add a new book to the catalogue.\n3. Remove book 15 from the catalogue (no body in the reply).\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `GET /books` → `200 OK`\n2. `POST /books` → `201 Created`\n3. `DELETE /books/15` → `204 No Content`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nWrite a complete raw HTTP/1.1 request that adds a volunteer to `clinic.example.org` at the path `/api/volunteers`. The body is the JSON `{\"name\":\"Hamza\",\"day\":\"Friday\"}`.\n1. Include the request line, `Host`, `Content-Type` and `Content-Length`.\n2. Write a sensible response with a status line, one header and a JSON body.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\nThe body `{\"name\":\"Hamza\",\"day\":\"Friday\"}` is 31 characters, so 31 bytes.\n\n```http\nPOST /api/volunteers HTTP/1.1\nHost: clinic.example.org\nContent-Type: application/json\nContent-Length: 31\n\n{\"name\":\"Hamza\",\"day\":\"Friday\"}\n```\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 201 Created\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"id\":7,\"name\":\"Hamza\",\"day\":\"Friday\"}\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-http-and-the-web/ch08-http-evolution-http1-http2-http3.md": "# Chapter 8: The Evolution of HTTP: HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 & HTTP/3 🚀\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAt first, the school canteen had one counter and one queue. If the student in front ordered lunch for the whole class, everyone behind waited. Then the canteen let one helper carry many trays at once, mixing small and big orders. Later, it opened separate paths, so one dropped tray no longer stopped every queue. HTTP grew up in the same three steps: **HTTP/1.1**, **HTTP/2** and **HTTP/3**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 became slow for modern pages.\n- Describe **head-of-line blocking**.\n- Explain how **HTTP/2** uses one connection for many requests (**multiplexing**).\n- Explain why **HTTP/3** moved from TCP to **QUIC** over UDP.\n- Find which HTTP version a website uses, with DevTools and `curl.exe`.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 A Short Timeline\n\nTim Berners-Lee proposed the World Wide Web in 1989. Early pages were simple documents with text and links. Today a single page may load a hundred files: images, scripts, fonts and stylesheets. HTTP had to change to keep up.\n\n| Year | Version | Big idea |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1996 | HTTP/1.0 | One TCP connection per file |\n| 1997 | HTTP/1.1 | Keep the connection open (`keep-alive`) |\n| 2015 | HTTP/2 | Binary frames, many requests at once |\n| 2022 | HTTP/3 | Runs over QUIC on UDP |\n\nThe *meaning* of HTTP did not change. Methods, status codes and headers (Chapter 7) are the same in every version. What changed is how the messages travel.\n\n---\n\n## ⏳ HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1: One at a Time\n\n**HTTP/1.0** opened a new TCP connection for every file. A page with 20 icons needed 20 handshakes (Chapter 4). That wasted time.\n\n**HTTP/1.1** added **persistent connections**. The header `Connection: keep-alive` keeps one TCP connection open for many requests, one after another.\n\n### The problem: head-of-line blocking\n\nOn one HTTP/1.1 connection, the responses come back **in order**, one at a time. If the first file is big, every small file behind it must wait. This is called **head-of-line blocking**: the item at the front of the line blocks everyone behind it.\n\n```\nOne HTTP/1.1 connection:\n[ big PDF ......... ][css][js][logo]\n  everything waits for the big PDF\n```\n\nTo get around this, browsers open about **six** connections to the same website. This helps, but each connection needs its own handshake and memory on the server.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ HTTP/2 (2015): Many Streams on One Connection\n\nHTTP/2 changed how the messages are packed:\n\n1. **Binary frames.** Messages are cut into small binary **frames** instead of plain text lines. Computers read them faster.\n2. **Multiplexing.** Each request and response gets its own **stream** number. Frames from many streams are mixed on **one** TCP connection, then sorted at the other end. Multiplexing means \"many signals sharing one line\".\n3. **Header compression (HPACK).** Headers repeat a lot, like `User-Agent` on every request. HPACK sends repeated headers as short codes.\n4. **Priority hints.** The browser can say which files matter most, such as the CSS needed to draw the page.\n\n```\nOne HTTP/2 connection:\n[css1][img1][js1][css2][img2][js2]\n frames from 3 streams, mixed\n```\n\nNow a big file no longer blocks small ones at the HTTP level.\n\n---\n\n## 🏎️ HTTP/3 (2022): QUIC over UDP\n\nHTTP/2 still had one hidden problem, in the layer below. It runs over **TCP**, and TCP delivers bytes strictly in order. If **one packet** is lost, TCP holds back *all* data behind it, for *every* stream, until the lost packet is resent. On a weak mobile signal this happens often.\n\n**HTTP/3** fixes this by leaving TCP. It runs on **QUIC**, a newer transport protocol built on top of **UDP** (Chapter 4). QUIC does its own resending and ordering, but **separately for each stream**.\n\n```\nHTTP/2 stack        HTTP/3 stack\n┌────────────┐      ┌────────────┐\n│  HTTP/2    │      │  HTTP/3    │\n├────────────┤      ├────────────┤\n│  TLS       │      │  QUIC      │\n├────────────┤      │ (with TLS) │\n│  TCP       │      ├────────────┤\n├────────────┤      │  UDP       │\n│  IP        │      ├────────────┤\n└────────────┘      │  IP        │\n                    └────────────┘\n```\n\nTLS is the encryption layer of HTTPS (Chapter 10). In QUIC, encryption is built in.\n\n### What HTTP/3 gives us\n\n1. **No transport head-of-line blocking.** A lost packet in stream A does not stop stream B.\n2. **Faster start.** QUIC sets up the connection and the encryption together, in one round trip. A returning visitor can sometimes send data in the very first packet. This is called **0-RTT** (zero round-trip time).\n3. **Connection migration.** A TCP connection is tied to your IP address and port. When your phone moves from school Wi-Fi to mobile data, the address changes and TCP connections break. QUIC names each connection with a **connection ID**, so it can carry on.\n\n---\n\n## 📊 Comparison\n\n| Feature | HTTP/1.1 | HTTP/2 | HTTP/3 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Transport | TCP | TCP | QUIC over UDP |\n| Format | Text | Binary frames | Binary frames |\n| Many requests on one connection | ❌ One at a time | ✅ Yes | ✅ Yes |\n| Header compression | ❌ None | ✅ HPACK | ✅ QPACK |\n| One lost packet stalls all streams | Yes | Yes (TCP) | ❌ No |\n| Survives a network change | ❌ No | ❌ No | ✅ Yes |\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: Which Version?\n\n### In the browser\n\n1. Open a website and press **F12** to open **DevTools**.\n2. Click the **Network** tab and reload the page.\n3. Right-click a column heading and tick **Protocol**.\n\nExample (sample rows; yours will differ):\n\n```\nName         Status  Protocol\nexample.org  200     h2\nstyle.css    200     h2\nlogo.png     200     h3\n```\n\n`http/1.1` means HTTP/1.1, `h2` means HTTP/2 and `h3` means HTTP/3.\n\n### With `curl.exe`\n\nHow does a browser learn that a server speaks HTTP/3? The server sends an `alt-svc` (alternative service) header. In PowerShell or Command Prompt:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -sI https://example.org | findstr /i alt-svc\n```\n\n- `-s` is silent mode; `-I` asks for headers only.\n- `findstr /i` keeps only lines containing `alt-svc`, ignoring upper or lower case.\n\nExample output:\n\n```\nalt-svc: h3=\":443\"; ma=86400\n```\n\nThis says: \"I also speak **h3** on UDP port **443**. Remember this for **86,400 seconds** (one day).\" The next visit can then use HTTP/3.\n\n> 💡 The `curl.exe` built into Windows only speaks HTTP/1.1. Its first line will say `HTTP/1.1 200 OK` even if browsers use HTTP/3. Use DevTools to see the real version.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Thinking HTTP/3 is unreliable because it uses UDP.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"HTTP/3 uses UDP, so web pages may arrive broken.\"\n- What happens: QUIC adds its own acknowledgements and resending on top of UDP.\n- ✅ Right: HTTP/3 is just as reliable as HTTP/2. It only handles losses per stream instead of for the whole connection.\n\n**Thinking a new HTTP version changes your code.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"I must rewrite my PHP pages for HTTP/2.\"\n- ✅ Right: methods, headers and status codes stay the same. The web server and browser choose the version for you.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOpen three different websites with DevTools' **Protocol** column turned on. For each, write down the protocols you see.\nThen run `curl.exe -sI https://example.org | findstr /i alt-svc`. Does the server offer HTTP/3?\n\n> 💡 Hint: the first visit may show `h2`, and a reload may show `h3`, because the browser learned about HTTP/3 from `alt-svc`.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- HTTP/1.0 opened a connection per file; HTTP/1.1 kept one open but answered one request at a time.\n- **Head-of-line blocking** means one slow item holds up everything behind it.\n- HTTP/2 mixes many streams on one TCP connection using binary frames.\n- HTTP/3 runs on QUIC over UDP, so one lost packet only delays its own stream.\n- QUIC connections survive a change of network using a connection ID.\n- Servers advertise HTTP/3 with the `alt-svc` header.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is **head-of-line blocking**? Which transport does HTTP/3 use instead of TCP?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nHead-of-line blocking is when the first item in a queue is slow and holds up every item behind it.\nHTTP/3 uses **QUIC**, which runs over **UDP**.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA server sends `alt-svc: h3=\":443\"; ma=3600`.\n1. Which HTTP version is it offering?\n2. On which port and which transport?\n3. For how long should the browser remember this?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. HTTP/3 (`h3`).\n2. Port 443 over UDP (QUIC runs on UDP).\n3. 3,600 seconds, which is one hour.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA student opens an online lesson page on a bus. The phone switches from bus Wi-Fi to mobile data halfway through.\n1. What happens to an HTTP/2 connection? Why?\n2. What happens to an HTTP/3 connection? Why?\n3. On a weak signal, one packet in ten is lost. Explain which version keeps the page's small files loading more smoothly, and why.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. The HTTP/2 connection breaks. TCP connections are tied to the phone's IP address and port, and the IP address changed. The browser must start a new connection.\n2. The HTTP/3 connection usually carries on. QUIC identifies it by a **connection ID**, not by the IP address.\n3. HTTP/3. With HTTP/2, each lost packet makes TCP hold back every stream. With HTTP/3, only the stream that lost the packet waits, so other files keep arriving.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-04-http-and-the-web/ch09-cookies-sessions-and-caching.md": "# Chapter 9: Statelessness, Cookies, Sessions & Web Caching 🍪\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nAt a school prize evening, guests leave their coats at the cloakroom. The helper hangs each coat on a numbered hook and gives the guest a small token with the same number. Later, the guest just shows the token, and the helper finds the coat at once. The helper does not need to remember any faces. On the web, the coat is your **session**, kept on the server, and the token is a **cookie**, kept in your browser.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what it means that HTTP is **stateless**.\n- Describe how a server sets a **cookie** and how the browser sends it back.\n- Explain how a **session** uses a cookie to remember a logged-in user.\n- Choose safe cookie flags: `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite`.\n- Use **caching** headers (`Cache-Control`, `ETag`, `Last-Modified`) and recognise `304 Not Modified`.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 HTTP Is Stateless\n\n**Stateless** means each HTTP request stands alone. The server does not remember the previous request from the same person.\n\nThat is simple and fast, but it causes a problem. You log in on page 1. You click to page 2. Without help, the server has no idea that you are the same person who just logged in.\n\nThe fix is **state management**: a way to carry \"who you are\" from one request to the next. Cookies are the main tool.\n\n---\n\n## 🍪 How Cookies Work\n\nA **cookie** is a small piece of text, up to about 4 KB, that a server asks the browser to store. The server sends it with the `Set-Cookie` response header. After that, the browser adds it to every request to the same site, in a `Cookie` request header.\n\n```\n1. Browser                 Server\n   POST /login  ───────────►\n   (name and password)\n2.            ◄─────────── 200 OK\n              Set-Cookie: sid=k7Q2xP\n3. GET /timetable ─────────►\n   Cookie: sid=k7Q2xP\n4.            ◄─────────── 200 OK\n              (Amina's timetable)\n```\n\nThe raw headers look like this:\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nSet-Cookie: sid=k7Q2xP; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\n```http\nGET /timetable HTTP/1.1\nHost: school.example.org\nCookie: sid=k7Q2xP\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🗄️ Sessions: The Server's Locker\n\nA **session** is a small record that the server keeps about one visitor, for example \"this visitor is Amina in class 8B\". The server gives the record a long random ID, the **session ID**. Only the ID goes into the cookie.\n\n```\nBrowser cookie        Server session store\nsid=k7Q2xP  ───────►  k7Q2xP → user: Amina\n                               class: 8B\n                      p3Lm9Z → user: Yusuf\n```\n\nThis is safer than putting the data itself in the cookie. A cookie can be read and changed by the person who holds it. A session stays on the server.\n\nBecause the session ID works like a key, it must stay secret. Anyone who steals it can pretend to be you.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ Safe Cookie Flags (*Amānah*)\n\nLogin cookies are a trust (*Amānah*). Protect them with these flags:\n\n| Flag | What it does | What it protects against |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `HttpOnly` | JavaScript on the page cannot read the cookie | **XSS**: an attacker's script injected into a page stealing the cookie |\n| `Secure` | Cookie is only sent over encrypted HTTPS (Chapter 10) | Someone on shared Wi-Fi reading it |\n| `SameSite=Lax` or `Strict` | Cookie is not sent with requests started by other websites | **CSRF**: another site tricking your browser into sending a request as you |\n\nAlso give cookies an end time with `Max-Age` (in seconds) or `Expires`, and delete the session when the user logs out.\n\n---\n\n## ⚡ Web Caching: Don't Download It Twice\n\nA **cache** is a saved copy. If the browser already has a file and it has not changed, downloading it again wastes data, time and electricity. HTTP has headers that tell browsers how long they may reuse a file.\n\n### `Cache-Control`\n\n```http\nCache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable\n```\n\n| Directive | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `max-age=31536000` | Reuse for 31,536,000 seconds (365 days) without asking |\n| `immutable` | This exact file will never change |\n| `no-cache` | You may keep a copy, but check with the server before each use |\n| `no-store` | Never save a copy anywhere (for private records like exam results) |\n| `private` | Only the user's own browser may save it, not shared caches |\n\nLong `max-age` values are used for files whose names change when their content changes, such as `app.3f9a1c.js`.\n\n### Checking a saved copy: validation\n\nWhen a cached copy is too old, the browser does not need to download it again. It can ask, \"Has this changed?\" This is a **conditional request**.\n\nThere are two kinds of fingerprints:\n\n- **ETag** (entity tag): a code that changes whenever the file changes, e.g. `ETag: \"v1-a9f2bc\"`. The browser asks with `If-None-Match: \"v1-a9f2bc\"`.\n- **Last-Modified**: the date the file last changed. The browser asks with `If-Modified-Since: <that date>`.\n\nIf nothing changed, the server replies **`304 Not Modified`** with **no body**. The browser uses its saved copy.\n\n```\nBrowser                       Server\n  │ GET /logo.png               │\n  │ If-None-Match: \"v1-a9f2bc\"  │\n  ├────────────────────────────►│\n  │ 304 Not Modified (no body)  │\n  │◄────────────────────────────┤\n```\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: A Real `304`\n\nFirst, ask for the headers and find `Last-Modified`. In PowerShell or Command Prompt:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -sI http://example.org | findstr /i \"cache-control last-modified\"\n```\n\nExample output (yours will differ):\n\n```\nlast-modified: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:19:47 GMT\nCache-Control: max-age=14400\n```\n\n`max-age=14400` means browsers may reuse the page for 4 hours. Now send that date back as a conditional request:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -sI -H \"If-Modified-Since: Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:19:47 GMT\" http://example.org\n```\n\n- `-H` adds your own request header.\n\nExample output (shortened):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified\nDate: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:42:02 GMT\nConnection: keep-alive\n```\n\nThe server sent no page at all, only \"your copy is still good\".\n\n### Cookies in the browser\n\nPress **F12**, open the **Application** tab (in Firefox: **Storage**), and choose **Cookies**. You will see each cookie's name, value, expiry and its `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite` settings.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Storing private data inside the cookie.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `Set-Cookie: user=Amina; role=teacher`\n- What happens: anyone can edit their own cookies in DevTools and change `role=student` to `role=teacher`.\n- ✅ Right: store only a random session ID in the cookie. Keep the user and role on the server.\n\n**Forgetting `HttpOnly` and `Secure` on a login cookie.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `Set-Cookie: sid=k7Q2xP`\n- ✅ Right: `Set-Cookie: sid=k7Q2xP; Path=/; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax`\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun the two `curl.exe` commands above. Use the `last-modified` date that *your* first command prints.\nDid you get `304 Not Modified`? Now change the date to one year earlier. What status do you get now, and why?\n\n> 💡 Hint: if the file changed *after* the date you send, the server must send the full page again.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- HTTP is **stateless**: each request stands alone.\n- A server sets a cookie with `Set-Cookie`; the browser sends it back with `Cookie`.\n- A **session** keeps user data on the server; the cookie only holds a random session ID.\n- Login cookies need `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite`.\n- `Cache-Control` sets how long a copy may be reused; `ETag` and `Last-Modified` let the browser check it.\n- `304 Not Modified` means \"use your saved copy\" and has no body.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhy does a website need cookies to keep you logged in? What does the `HttpOnly` flag do?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nBecause HTTP is **stateless**: the server does not remember earlier requests. The cookie carries a session ID with every request, so the server can recognise you.\n`HttpOnly` stops JavaScript on the page from reading the cookie, which protects it from theft by injected scripts (XSS).\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nChoose a `Cache-Control` value for each file:\n1. `app.3f9a1c.js`, whose name changes when its content changes.\n2. A student's exam results page.\n3. The school news page, which changes a few times a day.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`\n2. `Cache-Control: no-store`\n3. `Cache-Control: no-cache` (always check), or a short `max-age` such as `max-age=300`.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA library website has a login.\n1. Write the `Set-Cookie` header it should send after login. The session should last 2 hours.\n2. Write the request the browser sends next for `/my-books`.\n3. The logo has `ETag: \"logo-v4\"`. Write the conditional request header and the server's reply if the logo is unchanged.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Two hours = 7,200 seconds:\n\n```http\nSet-Cookie: sid=R8vT2q; Path=/; Max-Age=7200; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\n2. The browser adds the cookie:\n\n```http\nGET /my-books HTTP/1.1\nHost: library.example.org\nCookie: sid=R8vT2q\n```\n\n3. Conditional request and reply:\n\n```http\nGET /logo.png HTTP/1.1\nHost: library.example.org\nIf-None-Match: \"logo-v4\"\n```\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified\nETag: \"logo-v4\"\n```\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-securing-the-wire/ch10-tls-ssl-and-https.md": "# Chapter 10: TLS/SSL, HTTPS & Digital Trust (*Amānah*) 🔐\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA clinic needs to post a patient's test results to a hospital in another city. Written on an open postcard, every sorting worker could read them. So the clinic and the hospital agree on a shared secret code that nobody else knows, and write in that code. But first the clinic must be sure the reply really comes from the hospital, not an impostor. So the hospital shows an ID card stamped by a trusted office. **HTTPS** does both jobs: it agrees on a secret code, and it checks the ID card.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain why plain HTTP is unsafe on shared networks.\n- Tell **symmetric** from **asymmetric** cryptography and say why TLS uses both.\n- Describe the **TLS 1.3 handshake** in one round trip, using **key exchange** and **signatures**.\n- Explain how **certificates** and **Certificate Authorities** prove a website's identity.\n- Read a website's certificate with your browser and with PowerShell.\n\n---\n\n## 👀 Why Plain HTTP Is Not Enough\n\nHTTP (Chapter 7) is plain text. Every router and Wi-Fi access point on the way can read it. On a shared Wi-Fi network, a person with a **packet sniffer** (a program that records network traffic) could read:\n\n- passwords typed into login forms,\n- session cookies (Chapter 9), which let them pretend to be you,\n- private messages and records.\n\nThey could even **change** a page on its way to you. This is called a **man-in-the-middle attack**: someone secretly sits between you and the server.\n\n**HTTPS** is HTTP sent inside **TLS** (Transport Layer Security). TLS gives three protections:\n\n| Protection | Meaning |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Confidentiality** | Only you and the server can read the data |\n| **Integrity** | Any change on the way is detected |\n| **Authentication** | You know you are talking to the real server |\n\nHTTPS uses TCP port **443** (or UDP 443 with HTTP/3). **SSL** is TLS's old name. All SSL versions are broken and switched off today, but people still say \"SSL certificate\".\n\n---\n\n## 🔑 Two Kinds of Cryptography\n\n**Cryptography** is the science of keeping messages secret and checking that they are real.\n\n### Symmetric: one shared key\n\nBoth sides use the **same secret key** to lock (encrypt) and unlock (decrypt). Examples: **AES** and **ChaCha20**. It is very fast, so it is used for all the actual data.\n\nThe problem: how do two strangers get the same key, when every message between them can be read?\n\n### Asymmetric: a key pair\n\nEach side has **two keys** that belong together: a **private key**, kept secret, and a **public key**, shared with everyone. Asymmetric cryptography is slower, but it can do two clever things:\n\n1. **Key exchange.** Two sides each make a temporary key pair and swap only the public halves. Each side mixes its own private half with the other's public half. Both get the **same shared secret**. Someone watching sees only the public halves and cannot work it out. TLS 1.3 uses **ECDHE** (Elliptic-Curve Diffie-Hellman, Ephemeral) for this.\n2. **Signatures.** The owner of a private key can **sign** data. Anyone with the public key can check the signature. Only the real owner could have made it.\n\n### The paint-mixing picture\n\n```\n Client                  Server\n secret: red          secret: blue\n   │ + shared yellow       │\n   ▼                       ▼\n orange ───── swap ────► green\n   │                       │\n green + red    =    orange + blue\n   └──── same brown ───────┘\n```\n\nEach side mixes yellow paint with its own secret colour and swaps the result. Each then adds its own secret colour again. Both end up with the same brown. Someone watching sees yellow, orange and green, but cannot un-mix them to find brown. Real key exchange uses maths that is hard to undo instead of paint.\n\nTLS uses both kinds: **asymmetric** for key exchange and signatures at the start, then **symmetric** for the fast bulk data.\n\n---\n\n## 🤝 The TLS 1.3 Handshake\n\nTLS 1.3 sets up a secure connection in **one round trip** (1-RTT), right after the TCP handshake.\n\n```\n Client                     Server\n   │ ClientHello               │\n   │  cipher list + key share  │\n   ├──────────────────────────►│\n   │ ServerHello + key share   │\n   │ ── now encrypted: ──      │\n   │ Certificate               │\n   │ CertificateVerify (sign)  │\n   │ Finished                  │\n   │◄──────────────────────────┤\n   │ Finished                  │\n   ├──────────────────────────►│\n   │ ==== encrypted HTTP ====  │\n```\n\n1. **ClientHello.** The client lists the ciphers (encryption methods) it supports and sends its **key share**, the public half of its temporary key.\n2. **ServerHello.** The server picks a cipher and sends its own key share. Now both sides can work out the same secret. Everything after this point is encrypted.\n3. **Certificate.** The server sends its certificate, which contains its long-term public key.\n4. **CertificateVerify.** The server **signs** the handshake with its private key. This proves it really owns the certificate.\n5. **Finished.** Both sides send a check value over the whole handshake. If anyone changed a message, the check fails and the connection stops.\n\nThe temporary key pairs are thrown away afterwards. So even if the server's private key is stolen one day, old recorded conversations stay secret. This is called **forward secrecy**.\n\n---\n\n## 📜 Certificates and Certificate Authorities\n\nHow does your browser know the certificate really belongs to `example.org`?\n\n1. The website owner creates a key pair and sends a request to a **Certificate Authority** (CA). A CA is a trusted organisation that checks domain ownership.\n2. The CA checks that the owner controls the domain.\n3. The CA **signs** an **X.509 certificate**. It says: \"This public key belongs to `example.org`, valid until this date.\"\n4. Your operating system and browser come with a list of trusted **root CAs**. The browser follows the chain of signatures up to one of them.\n\n```\nRoot CA  (in your OS)\n   │ signs\nIntermediate CA\n   │ signs\nexample.org certificate\n```\n\nIf any check fails (wrong name, expired, unknown CA), the browser shows a full-page warning.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: Read a Certificate\n\n### In the browser\n\n1. Click the icon to the left of the address bar.\n2. Choose **Connection is secure**, then **Certificate is valid**.\n3. Look at **Issued To**, **Issued By** and **Valid to**.\n\n### In PowerShell\n\nThis short script connects to a site and prints its certificate details:\n\n```powershell\n$req = [Net.HttpWebRequest]::Create('https://example.org')\n$req.GetResponse().Close()\n$cert = $req.ServicePoint.Certificate\n$cert.Subject\n$cert.Issuer\n$cert.GetExpirationDateString()\n```\n\nExample output (issuer name and date are sample values; yours will differ):\n\n```\nCN=example.org\nCN=Example Trust Issuing CA 3, O=Example Trust Ltd, C=US\n26-12-2026 03:43:27\n```\n\n- `Subject` `CN=example.org` is the name the certificate protects. **CN** means Common Name.\n- `Issuer` is the CA that signed it.\n- The last line is the expiry date. Many certificates last only about three months and renew automatically.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ HTTPS as *Amānah*\n\nPeople trust us with their passwords, health records and donations. Keeping them private is an *Amānah*. Good practice:\n\n- Serve every page over HTTPS, not only the login page.\n- Never ask users to \"click through\" a certificate warning.\n- Renew certificates automatically, so they never expire by surprise.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Saying \"the browser encrypts the data with the server's public key\".**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"TLS encrypts my password with the website's public key.\"\n- What really happens: in TLS 1.3, the public key in the certificate is used to check a **signature**. The data is encrypted with a **symmetric key** that both sides worked out by key exchange.\n- ✅ Right: \"Key exchange makes a shared secret; signatures prove identity; symmetric encryption protects the data.\"\n\n**Thinking the padlock means the website is honest.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"It has HTTPS, so it is a safe, trustworthy site.\"\n- ✅ Right: the padlock only means the connection is private and the name matches. A dishonest site can have a valid certificate too. Always check the address.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nCheck the certificates of two websites your school uses, in the browser or with the PowerShell script.\nFor each, write the Subject, the Issuer and the expiry date. Which one expires sooner?\n\n> 💡 Hint: change the address inside `Create('...')` in the script. Keep the `https://`.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- HTTPS = HTTP inside TLS: confidentiality, integrity and authentication.\n- Symmetric keys are fast and protect the data; asymmetric key pairs do key exchange and signatures.\n- TLS 1.3 needs one round trip: ClientHello and ServerHello swap key shares, then the server proves its identity.\n- A **CA** signs certificates; browsers trust a built-in list of root CAs.\n- Temporary keys give **forward secrecy**.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhat is the difference between symmetric and asymmetric cryptography? Which one protects the bulk of the data in HTTPS?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nSymmetric cryptography uses **one shared secret key** for locking and unlocking. Asymmetric cryptography uses a **key pair**: a private key kept secret and a public key shared openly. It is used for key exchange and signatures.\nThe bulk data is protected by **symmetric** encryption, because it is much faster.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nPut these TLS 1.3 handshake messages in order and say who sends each:\n`Finished`, `ClientHello`, `CertificateVerify`, `ServerHello`, `Certificate`, `Finished`.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `ClientHello`: client\n2. `ServerHello`: server\n3. `Certificate`: server\n4. `CertificateVerify`: server\n5. `Finished`: server\n6. `Finished`: client\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA student connects to `library.example.org` on café Wi-Fi. An attacker on the same Wi-Fi tries three tricks. For each, explain which part of TLS stops it.\n1. Recording the traffic to read the student's password.\n2. Changing a page on its way to the student.\n3. Pretending to be `library.example.org` with a home-made certificate.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **Confidentiality.** The data is encrypted with a symmetric key from the key exchange. The attacker saw only the public key shares and cannot work out the key.\n2. **Integrity.** Every encrypted record carries a check value. A changed record fails the check and is rejected.\n3. **Authentication.** The home-made certificate is not signed by a CA in the browser's trusted list, so the browser shows a warning and stops. The attacker also cannot make a valid `CertificateVerify` signature for the real certificate without its private key.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-05-securing-the-wire/ch11-cors-and-web-security.md": "# Chapter 11: CORS, Same-Origin Policy & Web Security 🛡️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nIn a school, each class keeps its own register. A teacher from class 8A can walk into the office and ask for 8A's register. But a stranger cannot walk in and read 8B's register just because they are in the building. If the office of 8B *wants* to share its register with the library, it writes a note: \"The library may read this.\" Browsers follow the same rule. Pages may only read data from their own **origin**, unless the other server writes a permission note called **CORS**.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Define an **origin** as scheme + host + port.\n- Explain what the **Same-Origin Policy** blocks and why.\n- Read and write **CORS** headers such as `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.\n- Explain when the browser sends a **preflight** `OPTIONS` request.\n- Name three more security headers: **HSTS**, **CSP** and `nosniff`.\n\n---\n\n## 🤔 The Famous Console Error\n\nSooner or later, every web developer sees this in the browser console:\n\n```\nAccess to fetch at 'https://api.example.org/books'\nfrom origin 'http://localhost:3000' has been\nblocked by CORS policy: No 'Access-Control-Allow-\nOrigin' header is present on the requested resource.\n```\n\n`fetch` is the browser's JavaScript tool for sending HTTP requests from a page. This error is **not a bug in the browser**. It is a safety rule doing its job.\n\n---\n\n## 🔒 The Same-Origin Policy\n\nAn **origin** is made of three parts:\n\n**Origin = scheme + host + port**\n\n- **Scheme**: `http` or `https`.\n- **Host**: the full domain name, such as `school.example.org`.\n- **Port**: the port number (Chapter 5). If it is not written, `http` means 80 and `https` means 443.\n\nThe **Same-Origin Policy** (SOP) says: JavaScript on a page from one origin may **not read** responses from a different origin.\n\nWhy? Imagine you are logged in to your school portal in one tab. In another tab, you open a harmful page. Without SOP, that page's script could send a request to the portal. Your browser would attach your login cookie (Chapter 9), and the script could read your marks. SOP stops the script from reading the answer.\n\n### Same origin or not?\n\nCompared with `https://school.example.org/students`:\n\n| URL | Same origin? | Why |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `https://school.example.org/teachers` | ✅ Yes | Only the path differs |\n| `http://school.example.org/students` | ❌ No | Different scheme |\n| `https://api.school.example.org/` | ❌ No | Different host |\n| `https://school.example.org:8443/` | ❌ No | Different port (8443 vs 443) |\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 CORS: Permission to Share\n\nSometimes sharing is wanted. A school's web app at `https://school.example.org` may need data from its own API at `https://api.school.example.org`. These are different origins.\n\n**CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) lets the **server** give permission with response headers:\n\n```http\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://school.example.org\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type\n```\n\nThe browser reads these headers. If the page's origin is allowed, the script may read the response. If not, the browser hides the response and shows the console error.\n\n```\n Page (school.example.org)\n        │ fetch\n        ▼\n Browser ──request──► API server\n        ◄──response── + CORS headers?\n        │\n  allowed? ── yes ─► page reads data\n        └──── no ──► console error\n```\n\n### Simple requests and preflight\n\nSome requests are **simple**: a `GET`, `HEAD` or `POST` that uses only basic headers and a basic form-style `Content-Type`. The browser sends them straight away and checks the CORS headers on the reply.\n\nOther requests need a **preflight** first. A preflight is a small `OPTIONS` request that asks, \"Am I allowed to send this?\" It is needed when the request:\n\n- uses a method such as `PUT`, `PATCH` or `DELETE`,\n- sends `Content-Type: application/json`,\n- adds a custom header such as `Authorization`.\n\n```\n1. Preflight (browser asks)\n   OPTIONS /api/donations\n   Origin: https://school.example.org\n   Access-Control-Request-Method: POST\n   Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type\n\n2. Server answers\n   204 No Content\n   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://school.example.org\n   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST\n\n3. Browser sends the real POST\n```\n\n`Access-Control-Max-Age` tells the browser how many seconds it may remember the answer, so it does not ask before every request.\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: A Real CORS Server\n\nYou can watch CORS headers with a tiny PHP file and PHP's built-in web server. Save this as `api.php` in an empty folder:\n\n```php\n<?php\n$allowed = 'http://localhost:3000';\nif (($_SERVER['HTTP_ORIGIN'] ?? '') === $allowed) {\n    header(\"Access-Control-Allow-Origin: $allowed\");\n    header('Vary: Origin');\n}\nif ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'OPTIONS') {\n    header('Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST');\n    header('Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type');\n    header('Access-Control-Max-Age: 600');\n    http_response_code(204);\n    exit;\n}\nheader('Content-Type: application/json');\necho json_encode(['books' => 1250]);\n```\n\nIn that folder, start the server with `php -S localhost:8000`. In a **second** terminal, send a preflight as the browser would. `-X OPTIONS` sets the method, `-H` adds headers and `-i` shows response headers:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -i -X OPTIONS http://localhost:8000/api.php -H \"Origin: http://localhost:3000\" -H \"Access-Control-Request-Method: POST\"\n```\n\nOutput (shortened):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 204 No Content\nX-Powered-By: PHP/8.3.31\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:3000\nVary: Origin\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type\nAccess-Control-Max-Age: 600\n```\n\nNow pretend to be a page from another origin:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -i http://localhost:8000/api.php -H \"Origin: http://other.example\"\n```\n\nOutput (shortened):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nX-Powered-By: PHP/8.3.31\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"books\":1250}\n```\n\nThere is **no** `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` line. A browser would hide this response from the other page. But notice: `curl.exe` still printed the data. CORS is enforced by **browsers**, not by the server. That is why private data still needs a real login check on the server.\n\n---\n\n## 🛡️ More Security Headers\n\n| Header | What it does |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `Strict-Transport-Security` (HSTS) | Tells the browser: \"Only use HTTPS for this site from now on\" |\n| `Content-Security-Policy` (CSP) | Lists which places scripts, styles and images may load from; blocks injected scripts |\n| `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` | Stops the browser from guessing a file's type, so a text file is never run as a script |\n\nExample:\n\n```http\nStrict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\n```\n\n`'self'` means \"only my own origin\".\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Trying to fix CORS in the front-end code.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: adding `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to the `fetch` request in JavaScript.\n- What happens: nothing changes. The browser only trusts this header when it comes **from the server**.\n- ✅ Right: add the CORS headers to the API server's response.\n\n**Allowing everyone with a star.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on an API that returns private student data.\n- What happens: any website may read the public responses. Browsers refuse `*` for requests that carry cookies, so logins may also break.\n- ✅ Right: list the exact origins you trust, such as `https://school.example.org`.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun the PHP demo above. Then edit `$allowed` to `http://localhost:5173` and send the first `curl.exe` command again, still with `Origin: http://localhost:3000`.\nWhich header disappears? What would the browser do now?\n\n> 💡 Hint: the server only adds `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` when the `Origin` header matches `$allowed` exactly.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- An **origin** = scheme + host + port; all three must match.\n- The **Same-Origin Policy** stops scripts reading responses from other origins.\n- **CORS** headers from the server grant permission; the fix for a CORS error is on the server.\n- Non-simple requests (JSON, `Authorization`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) get an `OPTIONS` **preflight** first.\n- CORS is a browser rule, not a lock; servers still need real login checks.\n- HSTS, CSP and `nosniff` add extra layers of protection.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich three parts must match for two URLs to have the same origin? Which method does a preflight use?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nThe **scheme**, the **host** and the **port**.\nA preflight uses the **`OPTIONS`** method.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA page at `https://clinic.example.org` calls `https://api.clinic.example.org/visits` with `Content-Type: application/json`.\n1. Is this the same origin?\n2. Will the browser send a preflight? Why?\n3. Which header must the API's response include?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. No. The hosts are different (`api.clinic...` vs `clinic...`).\n2. Yes. `application/json` is not a simple content type, so the browser asks first with `OPTIONS`.\n3. `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://clinic.example.org`, plus `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type` in the preflight answer.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA charity runs its donation page at `https://give.example.org` and its API at `https://api.give.example.org`.\n1. Write the preflight response headers that allow only the donation page to `POST` JSON.\n2. Add three security headers to every API response.\n3. Explain why the API must still check the user's login even with CORS set correctly.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Preflight response:\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 204 No Content\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://give.example.org\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: POST\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type\nAccess-Control-Max-Age: 600\n```\n\n2. Security headers:\n\n```http\nStrict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\n```\n\n3. CORS only controls what **browsers** let pages read. Tools like `curl.exe` ignore it completely. Only a real login check on the server keeps donors' records safe.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-modern-web-and-diagnostics/ch12-rest-apis-and-realtime-websockets.md": "# Chapter 12: REST APIs & Real-Time WebSockets ⚡\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nThere are two ways to hear news from the school office. You can walk to the office window and ask, \"Any news?\" The clerk answers, and you walk away. If you want fresh news, you must keep walking back to ask. Or the office can give your class a walkie-talkie. Now either side can speak at any moment, without anyone walking anywhere. A **REST API** is the office window. A **WebSocket** is the walkie-talkie.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Explain what an **API** is and the main rules of **REST** design.\n- Design clean REST URLs using nouns and HTTP methods.\n- Explain why plain HTTP is awkward for live updates, and what **polling** costs.\n- Describe how a **WebSocket** starts with an HTTP upgrade and then stays open.\n- Choose between REST, WebSockets and **Server-Sent Events**.\n\n---\n\n## 🧩 What Is an API?\n\nAn **API** (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules that lets one program ask another program for data or actions. A **web API** does this over HTTP (Chapter 7). Instead of HTML pages, it usually sends data as **JSON**, a simple text format like `{\"title\":\"Book of Optics\"}`.\n\nA phone app, a website and a school display screen can all use the same API.\n\n---\n\n## 🏛️ REST: Representational State Transfer\n\n**REST** is a popular style for designing web APIs. It is not a new protocol. It simply uses HTTP the way HTTP was meant to be used.\n\n### Rule 1: URLs name things (nouns)\n\nEach thing the API manages is a **resource**, named by a URL.\n\n- ✅ `GET /api/books` — the list of books\n- ✅ `GET /api/books/42` — book number 42\n- ❌ `/api/getAllBooks` or `/api/deleteBook?id=42`\n\n### Rule 2: Methods say what to do (verbs)\n\n| Request | Meaning | Typical success code |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `GET /api/books` | List books | `200 OK` |\n| `GET /api/books/15` | Read book 15 | `200 OK` |\n| `POST /api/books` | Add a new book | `201 Created` |\n| `PUT /api/books/15` | Replace book 15 | `200 OK` |\n| `PATCH /api/books/15` | Change some fields | `200 OK` |\n| `DELETE /api/books/15` | Remove book 15 | `204 No Content` |\n\n### Rule 3: Stateless\n\nEach request carries everything the server needs, including who you are. For example, a login token in a header like `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The server does not rely on remembering the previous request (Chapter 9).\n\n---\n\n## 🔍 See It Yourself: A Tiny REST API\n\nPHP has a built-in web server, so you can run a real API on your own computer. Save this as `books.php` in an empty folder:\n\n```php\n<?php\nheader('Content-Type: application/json');\nif ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] === 'POST') {\n    $book = json_decode(file_get_contents('php://input'), true);\n    http_response_code(201);\n    echo json_encode(['id' => 3, 'title' => $book['title']]);\n    exit;\n}\necho json_encode([\n    ['id' => 1, 'title' => 'The Canon of Medicine'],\n    ['id' => 2, 'title' => 'Book of Optics'],\n]);\n```\n\nStart it with `php -S localhost:8000`. In a second terminal, read the list:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -i http://localhost:8000/books.php\n```\n\nOutput (shortened):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nX-Powered-By: PHP/8.3.31\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n[{\"id\":1,\"title\":\"The Canon of Medicine\"},{\"id\":2,\"title\":\"Book of Optics\"}]\n```\n\nNow add a book. Save the body `{\"title\":\"The Book of Healing\"}` in a file called `book.json` in the same folder, then run:\n\n```\ncurl.exe -i -X POST http://localhost:8000/books.php -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" --data-binary \"@book.json\"\n```\n\n- `-X POST` sets the method.\n- `--data-binary \"@book.json\"` sends the file as the body. Using a file avoids quoting problems in PowerShell.\n\nOutput (shortened):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 201 Created\nX-Powered-By: PHP/8.3.31\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\"id\":3,\"title\":\"The Book of Healing\"}\n```\n\nThis demo does not save anything; a real API would store the book in a database.\n\n---\n\n## ⏳ The Problem with Live Updates\n\nHTTP is **request–response**. The client asks; the server answers. The server cannot start a conversation on its own.\n\nImagine a library screen showing which study seats are free. With plain HTTP, the screen must ask again and again: \"Any change? Any change?\" This is called **polling**.\n\n```\nScreen            Server\n │ GET /seats ──►  │  no change\n │ GET /seats ──►  │  no change\n │ GET /seats ──►  │  seat 12 free!\n```\n\nPolling every 2 seconds sends full HTTP headers each time, even when nothing changed. With hundreds of screens, this wastes a lot of data and server work. And an update can still arrive up to 2 seconds late.\n\n---\n\n## 🔌 WebSockets: A Two-Way Line\n\nThe **WebSocket** protocol keeps one TCP connection open (Chapter 4). Once it is open, **both** sides can send messages at any time. This is called **full-duplex**: both directions work at the same time, like a phone call. The addresses start with `ws://`, or `wss://` for the encrypted version (Chapter 10).\n\n### Step 1: The upgrade request\n\nA WebSocket begins as a normal HTTP/1.1 request that asks to switch protocols:\n\n```http\nGET /seats HTTP/1.1\nHost: library.example.org\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==\nSec-WebSocket-Version: 13\n```\n\n### Step 2: The server agrees\n\n```http\nHTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Accept: s3pPLMBiTxaQ9kYGzzhZRbK+xOo=\n```\n\n`101 Switching Protocols` means \"yes, let's switch\". The server builds `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` from the client's key using a fixed recipe. This proves it really understands WebSockets. (We checked this pair with Python's `hashlib`.)\n\n### Step 3: Messages in frames\n\nFrom now on, HTTP is no longer used on this connection. Messages travel in small **frames**. Each frame adds only **2 to 14 bytes** of header, depending on the message size and direction. Messages from the browser add a 4-byte \"mask\" for safety.\n\n```\nScreen                Server\n  │◄── {\"seat\":12,\"free\":true}\n  │◄── {\"seat\":7,\"free\":false}\n  │── {\"hello\":\"screen-3\"} ──►\n```\n\nA 23-byte update like `{\"seat\":12,\"free\":true}` needs only about 2 extra bytes, instead of hundreds of bytes of HTTP headers.\n\nIn DevTools (**F12**), the **Network** tab has a **WS** filter. Click a WebSocket and open **Messages** to see each frame.\n\n---\n\n## 📡 Which One Should I Use?\n\n| Technology | Direction | Connection | Good for |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **REST** (HTTP) | Client asks, server answers | Short, or kept alive | Forms, lists, saving records |\n| **WebSocket** | Both ways, any time | One long-lived TCP connection | Shared class whiteboard, live chat for a study group |\n| **Server-Sent Events** (SSE) | Server → client only | One long-lived HTTP response | Flood alerts, bus arrival boards, notice boards |\n\n**Server-Sent Events** keep one HTTP response open and let the server add new events to it. It is simpler than WebSockets when only the server needs to talk.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Putting verbs in REST URLs.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `POST /api/createBook`, `GET /api/deleteBook?id=15`\n- What happens: the method and the URL disagree. A GET that deletes is dangerous, because links are followed freely.\n- ✅ Right: `POST /api/books` and `DELETE /api/books/15`.\n\n**Using a WebSocket for everything.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: loading the whole page, forms and images over a WebSocket.\n- ✅ Right: use REST for normal requests. Use a WebSocket only for live, two-way updates. It needs a server that keeps many connections open.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nRun the `books.php` demo. Then change it so that a `GET` request returns three books instead of two, and check the change with `curl.exe -i`.\n\n> 💡 Hint: add one more `['id' => 3, 'title' => '...']` line inside the list. Use a real book title from the history of science.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- An **API** lets programs talk; web APIs usually send JSON over HTTP.\n- **REST** uses nouns in URLs and HTTP methods as verbs, and every request stands alone.\n- **Polling** repeats requests and wastes data.\n- A **WebSocket** starts with `Upgrade: websocket` and `101 Switching Protocols`, then sends small frames both ways.\n- WebSocket frames add only 2–14 bytes each.\n- Choose REST for normal requests, WebSockets for two-way live data and SSE for server-only updates.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhy do REST URLs use nouns instead of verbs? Which status code means a WebSocket upgrade succeeded?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nBecause the **HTTP method** (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) already says *what to do*. The URL only needs to say *which thing*.\nA successful upgrade returns **`101 Switching Protocols`**.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nWrite the REST request (method and URL) for each action on a community clean-up app:\n1. List all clean-up events.\n2. Create a new event.\n3. Change only the meeting time of event 8.\n4. Cancel (remove) event 8.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `GET /api/events`\n2. `POST /api/events`\n3. `PATCH /api/events/8`\n4. `DELETE /api/events/8`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA town wants a bus arrival board at the school gate and a live question channel between a teacher and students in an online lesson.\n1. For each, choose REST polling, WebSocket or SSE, and give a reason.\n2. The board polls every 5 seconds with 400 bytes of headers per request. How many bytes of headers does it send in one hour?\n3. Explain how your choice for the board would reduce this.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. **Bus board: SSE.** Only the server needs to send updates. **Question channel: WebSocket.** Teacher and students both send messages at any time.\n2. One hour = 3,600 seconds ÷ 5 = 720 requests. 720 × 400 = **288,000 bytes** of headers (about 288 KB), even if nothing changed.\n3. With SSE, the board opens one connection once. After that, the server only sends a small event when a bus time actually changes.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-modern-web-and-diagnostics/ch13-network-diagnostics-and-devtools.md": "# Chapter 13: Network Diagnostics & Developer Tools 🛠️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nWhen a student feels unwell, the school nurse does not guess. She checks temperature, then pulse, then asks questions, one step at a time, starting with the simplest test. Network engineers work the same way. When \"the Internet is not working\", they run a few small tests in order. Each test rules something out, until only the real cause is left.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Use `ipconfig`, `ping`, `tracert`, `nslookup`, `curl.exe` and `netstat` on Windows.\n- Explain **TTL** and how `tracert` uses it to map a route.\n- Follow a step-by-step troubleshooting ladder from the bottom layer up.\n- Read the browser's **DevTools Network** panel and its **waterfall**.\n- Test responsibly: only on networks you own or have permission to test.\n\n---\n\n## 🛑 First: The Permission Rule\n\nDiagnostic tools are like a doctor's tools: helpful in the right hands. You must **only** test your own devices, your home network, or your school lab network when your teacher allows it. Probing or scanning other people's networks without written permission is wrong, and in many countries it is against the law. Honest testing is part of our *Amānah*.\n\n---\n\n## 🪜 The Troubleshooting Ladder\n\nStart at the bottom layer (Chapter 2). Only move up when a step works.\n\n```\n 5. curl.exe   Is the web server OK?  (7)\n 4. nslookup   Does the name work?    (7)\n 3. ping       Can I reach far away?  (3)\n 2. ping gw    Can I reach my router? (3)\n 1. ipconfig   Do I have an address?  (3)\n 0. Look       Cable in? Wi-Fi on?    (1)\n```\n\nThe number in brackets is the OSI layer.\n\n---\n\n## 💻 The Tools, One by One\n\n### `ipconfig`: Do I have an address?\n\n```\nipconfig\n```\n\nExample output (shortened; yours will differ):\n\n```\nWireless LAN adapter Wi-Fi:\n\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.1.7\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.1.1\n```\n\nIf the IPv4 address starts with `169.254`, your computer gave itself an address because no router answered. Check the Wi-Fi or cable. `ipconfig /all` shows more detail, such as your DNS servers.\n\n### `ping`: Can I reach it?\n\nFirst ping your **default gateway** (your router), then something far away:\n\n```\nping 192.168.1.1\nping example.org\n```\n\nExample output for the second one (addresses changed to documentation addresses):\n\n```\nPinging example.org [203.0.113.10] with 32 bytes of data:\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=20ms TTL=60\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=19ms TTL=60\nRequest timed out.\nReply from 203.0.113.10: bytes=32 time=21ms TTL=60\n\nPing statistics for 203.0.113.10:\n    Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 3, Lost = 1 (25% loss),\n```\n\n- `time=` is the **round-trip time**: there and back.\n- `Request timed out.` means one reply did not come back in time.\n- `25% loss` here means 1 of 4 packets was lost. Steady loss suggests a weak or busy link.\n\n### What is TTL?\n\nEvery IP packet carries a **TTL** (Time To Live) number. Each router that forwards the packet takes 1 away. If TTL reaches 0, the router throws the packet away and sends back a short \"time exceeded\" message. This stops lost packets from looping forever.\n\nComputers start packets with a TTL such as 64 or 128. `TTL=60` in the reply suggests the reply started at 64 and passed through about 4 routers.\n\n### `tracert`: Which way do packets go?\n\n`tracert` (trace route) sends packets with TTL 1, then 2, then 3, and so on. Each router where the TTL runs out replies with \"time exceeded\". So, hop by hop, `tracert` learns every router on the path.\n\n```\ntracert -d example.org\n```\n\n- `-d` skips looking up router names, so it is much faster.\n\nExample output (addresses changed to documentation and sample addresses):\n\n```\nTracing route to example.org [203.0.113.10]\nover a maximum of 30 hops:\n\n  1     3 ms     5 ms     2 ms  192.168.1.1\n  2     5 ms     3 ms     2 ms  10.240.15.104\n  3     *        *        *     Request timed out.\n  4    18 ms    17 ms    18 ms  198.51.100.39\n  5    20 ms    19 ms    20 ms  203.0.113.10\n\nTrace complete.\n```\n\n- Hop 1 is your router. Hop 2 is often inside your ISP.\n- Each hop is tested three times, so there are three times.\n- `*` means that router did not reply. Many routers are set to stay quiet. If later hops answer, the path is fine.\n\nOn macOS and Linux, the command is `traceroute`.\n\n### `nslookup`: Does the name work?\n\n```\nnslookup example.org\n```\n\nIf you get an address back, DNS works (Chapter 6). If `ping 203.0.113.10` works but `ping example.org` does not, the problem is DNS, not the connection.\n\n### `curl.exe`: Is the web server healthy?\n\nRemember to type `curl.exe`, not `curl`, in Windows PowerShell 5 (Chapter 7).\n\n```\ncurl.exe -I https://example.org\n```\n\nExample output (shortened):\n\n```\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nContent-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8\nCache-Control: max-age=14400\n```\n\nA `200` means the web server answered properly. A `5xx` code means the network is fine but the server program has a problem.\n\n### `netstat`: What is my computer doing?\n\n```\nnetstat -ano | findstr LISTENING\n```\n\nExample output (shortened):\n\n```\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:80     0.0.0.0:0    LISTENING    9096\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:443    0.0.0.0:0    LISTENING    9096\n  TCP    0.0.0.0:3306   0.0.0.0:0    LISTENING    7120\n```\n\nThis shows the ports your own computer is listening on, and the PID of each program (Chapter 5).\n\n### A PowerShell shortcut\n\n`Test-NetConnection` checks several things in one go:\n\n```\nTest-NetConnection example.org -Port 443\n```\n\nThe last line, `TcpTestSucceeded : True`, means the TCP handshake on port 443 worked.\n\n---\n\n## 🌐 The Browser's Network Panel\n\nPress **F12** and open the **Network** tab, then reload the page.\n\n```\nName       Status  Type    Size    Time\nindex      200     doc     12 kB   45 ms\napp.js     200     script  (disk)  2 ms\nbooks      201     fetch   1 kB   110 ms\n```\n\n- **Status** is the HTTP status code (Chapter 7).\n- **Size** `(disk cache)` means the file came from the browser's cache (Chapter 9).\n- **Filter** buttons show only `Fetch/XHR` (API calls), `JS`, `CSS`, `Img` or `WS` (WebSockets).\n\nHover over a row's **Waterfall** bar to see where the time went:\n\n| Part | What it measures |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| DNS Lookup | Turning the name into an address |\n| Initial connection | The TCP handshake |\n| SSL | The TLS handshake |\n| Waiting for server response (TTFB) | **Time To First Byte**: from sending the request until the first byte of the reply |\n| Content Download | Receiving the rest of the reply |\n\nTwo more useful settings:\n\n- **Throttling** (the \"No throttling\" menu) lets you pretend to be on a slow connection, such as 3G. Use it to test your pages for users in rural areas.\n- **Preserve log** keeps the list when the page reloads or redirects.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Deciding a website is down because ping fails.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: `ping example.org` shows `Request timed out.` four times, so \"the website is down\".\n- What happens: many servers and firewalls block ping replies on purpose.\n- ✅ Right: also try `curl.exe -I` or the browser. Ping only tests one kind of packet.\n\n**Panicking at `*` in tracert.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: \"Hop 3 shows `* * *`, so the route is broken there.\"\n- ✅ Right: if later hops reply, packets are getting through. That router simply does not answer tracert.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nOn your home network, or your school lab with your teacher's permission, climb the ladder:\n\n1. `ipconfig` — write down your IPv4 address and default gateway.\n2. `ping` your gateway, then `ping example.org`.\n3. `tracert -d example.org` — how many hops did it take?\n4. `curl.exe -I https://example.org` — what status code did you get?\n\n> 💡 Hint: if any step fails, stop there. That is the layer to investigate.\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Always get permission before testing a network you do not own.\n- Troubleshoot bottom-up: link, address, gateway, far host, DNS, web server.\n- `ping` measures round-trip time and loss; `tracert` maps the route using **TTL**.\n- `nslookup` tests DNS; `curl.exe -I` tests the web server; `netstat -ano` shows local ports.\n- DevTools' Network panel shows status, size, cache use and a timing **waterfall** including TTFB.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nWhich Windows command maps the routers between you and a server? What does **TTFB** stand for?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n**`tracert`** (on macOS/Linux: `traceroute`).\n**TTFB** = Time To First Byte: the time from sending the request until the first byte of the response arrives.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nA student reports \"no Internet\". You find:\n- `ipconfig` shows `IPv4 Address: 192.168.1.7` and gateway `192.168.1.1`.\n- `ping 192.168.1.1` works.\n- `ping 203.0.113.10` works.\n- `ping example.org` says `Ping request could not find host example.org`.\n\nWhich layer or service is failing, and what would you check next?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\nThe connection works all the way to a far IP address, so the network is fine. The **name** cannot be turned into an address, so **DNS** is failing.\nNext, run `nslookup example.org` and check the DNS servers in `ipconfig /all`. Clearing the cache with `ipconfig /flushdns` may also help.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nA charity's donation page feels slow. In DevTools, the main request shows: DNS Lookup 5 ms, Initial connection 30 ms, SSL 35 ms, Waiting (TTFB) 1,800 ms, Content Download 20 ms.\n1. Add up the total time.\n2. Which part is the problem? Is it the network or the server?\n3. Suggest two things the developers could check.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. 5 + 30 + 35 + 1,800 + 20 = **1,890 ms**, almost 2 seconds.\n2. **TTFB** (1,800 ms) is the problem. DNS, TCP and TLS are all quick, so the network is fine. The **server** is slow to start answering.\n3. Check whether a slow database query runs on every page load, and whether the page could be cached (Chapter 9) instead of being built again each time.\n\n</details>\n",
  "part-06-modern-web-and-diagnostics/ch14-capstone-resilient-community-network.md": "# Chapter 14: Capstone: Architecting a Resilient Community Network 🏗️\n\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\n\nA town is building a new community centre. The architect does not start with bricks. She first asks: who will use it, what must it survive, and where should the doors and locks go? Only then does she draw the plan, room by room. In this capstone, you are the architect. You will plan the network for a town's school, library and charity clinic, using every idea from this book.\n\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\n\n- Turn real needs (speed, safety, slow connections, failures) into network design choices.\n- Plan addresses and subnets for a small cloud network using CIDR.\n- Explain, in plain words, the building blocks of a real website: load balancer, bastion host, NAT, CDN and firewall.\n- Write the DNS records, HTTPS settings and firewall rules for the design.\n- Plan how to diagnose problems when something goes wrong.\n\n---\n\n## 🎯 The Mission: The Oasis Community Portal\n\nThe town of Oasis has a school, a public library and a small charity clinic. They want one website, `oasis.example.org`, where:\n\n- students check timetables and library books,\n- families book clinic appointments,\n- donors give to the clinic's clean-water fund.\n\n### The four requirements\n\n1. **Always available.** If one server breaks, the site must keep working.\n2. **Works on slow connections.** Many families use weak mobile signals.\n3. **Private and safe (*Amānah*).** Health records and donor details must never travel or sit unprotected.\n4. **Easy to fix.** When something goes wrong, staff must be able to find the cause quickly.\n\n---\n\n## 📖 The Building Blocks, in Plain Words\n\nReal websites use a few extra parts. Each one is explained in one sentence here. The rest of the chapter shows where each one fits.\n\n| Term | What it is, in one sentence |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Cloud network (VPC)** | A **Virtual Private Cloud** is a private, fenced-off network that you rent inside a cloud provider's data centre. |\n| **Subnet** | A smaller slice of a network's addresses, made with CIDR, like `10.20.1.0/24` (Chapter 3). |\n| **Load balancer** | A server that receives every visitor first and shares them out between several web servers, so no single one is overloaded. |\n| **Reverse proxy** | A server that answers the public on behalf of the hidden servers behind it; a load balancer is one kind. |\n| **Bastion host** | One specially guarded computer that staff must log in to first, before they can reach any other server. |\n| **NAT gateway** | A device that lets private servers send requests out to the Internet, for example to download updates, while no one outside can start a connection in (Chapter 3). |\n| **Firewall** | A set of rules that allows or blocks traffic by address and port (Chapter 5). |\n| **WAF** | A **Web Application Firewall** reads HTTP requests and blocks known attack patterns before they reach the website. |\n| **CDN** | A **Content Delivery Network** keeps copies of images, CSS and scripts on servers in many cities, so each visitor downloads them from somewhere nearby. |\n| **Anycast** | Many servers share one IP address, and each visitor is routed to the nearest one (Chapter 6). |\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ The Big Picture\n\n```\n Families, students, donors\n            │ HTTPS\n            ▼\n   [ CDN: images, CSS, JS ]\n            │\n            ▼\n [ Load balancer + WAF ]\n   public 203.0.113.20\n       ┌────┴────┐\n       ▼         ▼\n [ Web A ]   [ Web B ]\n       └────┬────┘\n            ▼\n     [ Database ]\n```\n\nVisitors only ever talk to the CDN and the load balancer. The web servers and the database have **no public addresses**. Two web servers mean that if Web A fails, the load balancer sends everyone to Web B.\n\n---\n\n## 🛠️ Step-by-Step Blueprint\n\n### Step 1: Addresses and subnets\n\nThe cloud network gets the private block `10.20.0.0/16`. A /16 has 65,536 addresses. We cut it into /24 subnets of 256 addresses each, one for each job:\n\n| Subnet | CIDR | What lives here | Public? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Public | `10.20.1.0/24` | Load balancer, bastion host, NAT gateway | Faces the Internet |\n| Web | `10.20.2.0/24` | Web A, Web B | Private |\n| Data | `10.20.3.0/24` | Database | Private |\n\n```\n10.20.0.0/16  (cloud network)\n ├─ 10.20.1.0/24  public\n ├─ 10.20.2.0/24  web\n └─ 10.20.3.0/24  data\n```\n\nSplitting by job lets us put a different firewall rule around each subnet. The database sits deepest, two walls away from the Internet.\n\n### Step 2: Firewall rules\n\nAllow only what is needed. Block everything else.\n\n| From | To | Port | Why |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Internet | Load balancer | TCP 443, UDP 443 | HTTPS and HTTP/3 |\n| Internet | Load balancer | TCP 80 | Only to redirect to HTTPS |\n| Load balancer | Web subnet | TCP 443 | Pass visitors on |\n| Web subnet | Data subnet | TCP 3306 | Web servers read the database |\n| School office `198.51.100.7` | Bastion host | TCP 22 | Staff log in with SSH |\n| Bastion host | Web and data subnets | TCP 22 | Staff maintenance |\n\nThe database port `3306` is **never** open to the Internet (Chapter 5).\n\n### Step 3: DNS records\n\n```\noasis.example.org      A      203.0.113.20\noasis.example.org      AAAA   2001:db8:20::20\nwww.oasis.example.org  CNAME  oasis.example.org\noasis.example.org      MX 10  mail.oasis.example.org\n```\n\nA TTL of `3600` is fine day to day. Lower it to `300` a day before any planned move (Chapter 6).\n\n### Step 4: HTTPS everywhere\n\n- The load balancer holds a TLS certificate for `oasis.example.org` and renews it automatically (Chapter 10).\n- Port 80 only sends a `301 Moved Permanently` to the HTTPS address (Chapter 7).\n- HTTP/3 is switched on, so phones that move between Wi-Fi and mobile data keep their connection (Chapter 8).\n\nSecurity headers on every response (Chapter 11):\n\n```http\nStrict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nSet-Cookie: sid=...; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax\n```\n\n### Step 5: Fast on slow connections\n\n- **Compression.** The server compresses text with gzip or Brotli, so pages and JSON become much smaller.\n- **Caching.** Files like `app.3f9a1c.js` get `Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable`. Clinic records get `no-store` (Chapter 9).\n- **CDN.** Images and scripts come from a nearby CDN server, so they travel a shorter distance.\n- **Offline reading.** The timetable page can be saved on the phone by a small browser script called a **service worker**, so it opens even with no signal.\n\n### Step 6: A plan for problems\n\nWhen the clinic says \"the site is slow\", staff follow the ladder from Chapter 13:\n\n1. `ping` and `tracert -d oasis.example.org` from the clinic: is the path OK?\n2. `nslookup oasis.example.org`: does the name give `203.0.113.20`?\n3. `curl.exe -I https://oasis.example.org`: what status code comes back?\n4. DevTools waterfall: is the delay in the network or in **TTFB** (the server)?\n\n---\n\n## 🌟 The Moral Dimension of Network Engineering\n\nNetwork engineering is not just moving bits between chips. It is building bridges of trust between people.\n\nWhen you shrink a page so a family on a weak signal can read a flood warning, when you keep health records behind three walls, and when you lock donors' details inside HTTPS, you are practising *Amānah* (trust) and *Iḥsān* (excellence). May your knowledge be a continuing benefit to others (*'Ilm Nāfi'*).\n\n## 🎓 Textbook Completion Milestone\n\nCongratulations! You have finished all 14 chapters of **Computer Networking & Web Protocols**. Next, try the interactive **Network Lab**, the practice exercises and the quiz in the Learning Networks track.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\n\n**Giving every server a public address.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: Web A, Web B and the database each get a public IP \"to make things easy\".\n- What happens: anyone on the Internet can knock on the database port and try passwords.\n- ✅ Right: only the load balancer (and the bastion host, for one office address) faces the Internet. Everything else stays private.\n\n**Having only one of everything.**\n\n- ❌ Wrong: one web server with no spare.\n- What happens: one broken disk takes the whole portal offline.\n- ✅ Right: at least two web servers behind a load balancer, plus regular database backups.\n\n---\n\n## ✍️ Try It\n\nDraw the Oasis design from memory on paper, as a text diagram no wider than your notebook page.\nLabel each box with its subnet, and mark every arrow with its port number.\n\n> 💡 Hint: there are three subnets and five arrows that need ports: 443 (twice), 3306 and 22 (twice).\n\n---\n\n## 📝 Recap\n\n- Start a design from needs: availability, slow connections, privacy and easy fixing.\n- Split a private cloud network (VPC) into subnets by job, and guard each with firewall rules.\n- Only the load balancer and a guarded bastion host face the Internet.\n- DNS, HTTPS, HTTP/3, security headers and caching all play a part.\n- Plan diagnostics before trouble starts.\n\n---\n\n## 🏆 Practice\n\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\nIn one sentence each, what is a **load balancer**, a **bastion host** and a **CDN**?\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n- A **load balancer** receives every visitor first and shares them between several servers.\n- A **bastion host** is one guarded computer that staff log in to before they can reach any other server.\n- A **CDN** keeps copies of files on servers in many places, so visitors download from somewhere nearby.\n\n</details>\n\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\nThe Oasis team wants a fourth subnet for a reporting server, inside `10.20.0.0/16`.\n1. Give a suitable /24 subnet that does not overlap the others.\n2. How many usable host addresses does it have?\n3. Write one firewall rule so the reporting server can read the database.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\n\n1. `10.20.4.0/24` (any unused `10.20.X.0/24` works).\n2. 256 − 2 = **254** usable hosts.\n3. `ALLOW  10.20.4.0/24 → 10.20.3.0/24  TCP 3306`\n\n</details>\n\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\nDesign a smaller network for a village library with a reading room and a staff office.\n1. Choose a private block and split it into two subnets. Give each subnet's CIDR and usable host range.\n2. List three firewall rules.\n3. Write the A record for `library.example.org` using a documentation address.\n4. Give two ways to make the library website fast on slow mobile connections.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\n\n1. Block `192.168.50.0/24`, split into two /25 subnets:\n\n```\n192.168.50.0/25    hosts .1 – .126    reading room\n192.168.50.128/25  hosts .129 – .254  staff office\n```\n\n2. Firewall rules:\n\n```\nALLOW  staff  → Internet       TCP 443\nALLOW  reading room → Internet TCP 443\nBLOCK  reading room → staff    all ports\n```\n\n3. `library.example.org  A  203.0.113.50`\n4. Compress text responses with gzip or Brotli, and give images and scripts long `Cache-Control` lifetimes so returning visitors do not download them again.\n\n</details>\n"
};
